// Generated by tools/build-help.mjs from the website's docs-content.js. Do not edit: edit the site's articles and run the builder again.
window.MOHelpData = {"site":"https://lukagrcar.com/","docs":"https://lukagrcar.com/apps/meshoptimiser/docs.html","groups":[{"id":"start","title":"Start here","blurb":"What the app is, what it needs, and how to get from a download to your first model on screen."},{"id":"cad","title":"Getting your model out of your CAD program","blurb":"MeshOptimiser opens STEP files. How to save one from Inventor, Onshape, Creo and Rhino, with the options that matter."},{"id":"around","title":"Find your way around","blurb":"The window, the mouse, selecting, hiding, the parts tree, search, undo, tabs and settings."},{"id":"lighter","title":"Make it lighter","blurb":"The tools that remove triangles and parts, one at a time, and a sensible order to use them in."},{"id":"look","title":"Look and arrange","blurb":"View modes, materials, moving and measuring, standing a model on the floor, and adding parts."},{"id":"save","title":"Save and export","blurb":"Keep your work as a scene, or write a file for another program."},{"id":"jobs","title":"Common jobs, start to finish","blurb":"Three jobs people come here to do: convert a STEP file to GLB, take the bolts out of an assembly, and make a model safe to send to a customer."},{"id":"reference","title":"Reference","blurb":"Every shortcut, every command-line option, the file formats and what is in the folder."},{"id":"help","title":"Help","blurb":"What to do when something goes wrong, and answers to common questions."}],"articles":[{"slug":"what-it-is","group":"start","title":"What MeshOptimiser is","summary":"A free app that runs on your own computer. It turns a heavy CAD file into a light 3D file and lets you inspect, clean up and export it.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser is a free, open-source app that runs on your own computer. You give it a heavy 3D file from an engineering program. It gives you back a much lighter file that a web page or another 3D program can load quickly, and a viewer to look at the model, clean it up and export it."],["p","You do not need to know anything about 3D to start. These pages explain each word the first time it is used, and [Words you will meet](#glossary) lists them all in one place."],["h","The problem it solves"],["p","Engineers design machines in CAD programs (CAD stands for computer-aided design). They usually hand the design over as a STEP file, a common exchange format for CAD. A STEP file describes every part exactly, down to the last screw, so it is often very large and slow to show."],["p","A real assembly (a model made of many parts) can hold 400 identical bolts and 80 identical brackets. MeshOptimiser notices the parts that are the same and stores each shape once, with a list of the places where it sits. It also turns the exact CAD surfaces into triangles, which is what screens draw, and can leave out parts that are too small to see."],["terms",[["400 bolts × 50 KB","Become 1 shape of 50 KB and 400 positions."],["80 brackets × 12 KB","Become 1 shape of 12 KB and 80 positions."],["A 320 MB STEP file","Becomes an 11 MB compressed GLB. This is the example from the app’s own README, not a promise for every file."]]],["h","The two halves"],["list",["The converter. A small Python program, `step2glb.py`, reads a STEP file and writes a GLB file. GLB is a compact 3D format that browsers and most 3D programs can open.","The viewer. A page that opens in a window of its own and shows the model. Here you look around, select parts, remove what you do not need, reduce triangles, recolour, and export."]],["p","You do not have to run the two halves yourself. One launcher starts everything, and you drop a file on the window."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-viewer.webp","A rear derailleur in 43 parts and 1.1 million triangles: the parts tree on the left, the scene’s totals on the right."],["h","What you can do with it"],["list",["Open STEP, GLB, glTF, FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files.","See how heavy a model is, and which parts make it heavy.","Delete parts nobody will see, close holes, simplify parts and reduce triangles.","Rename, group and tidy the list of parts.","Recolour materials, move parts, measure distances and add standard parts from a library.","Save your work, and export to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY, or a parts list."]],["h","What it is not"],["list",["It is not a CAD program. It reads STEP files but does not write them, and it does not edit exact CAD surfaces.","It is not an online service. Nothing is uploaded to the internet. See [Privacy and offline use](#privacy-offline).","It is not finished. The version is below 1.0, and things may change between versions."]],["note","MeshOptimiser runs on Windows and macOS, is free, and is published under the MIT licence."],["see",["glossary","requirements","first-start","cad-files-step-first"]]]},{"slug":"glossary","group":"start","title":"Words you will meet","summary":"A plain-language glossary: STEP, mesh, triangle, GLB, instancing, decimation, WebGPU, virtual environment and the other terms these pages use.","blocks":[["p","You do not need to learn these before you start. Come back to this page when a word is new."],["h","Files and formats"],["terms",[["CAD","Computer-aided design. The programs engineers use to design real objects, such as SolidWorks or NX."],["STEP (.step, .stp)","A file format for exchanging CAD designs between programs. It stores exact surfaces, part names, colours and how the parts fit together."],["GLB","A compact 3D file made of triangles, with colours and part names inside one file. Browsers and most 3D programs can open it. This is the format MeshOptimiser writes by default."],["glTF","The same format as GLB, written as readable text. The app’s own format sheet describes it as the same capabilities, larger."],["FBX, OBJ, STL, PLY, USDZ, 3MF","Other 3D file formats. [File formats](#file-formats) says what each one is for."],["XCAF","The part of a STEP file that holds names, colours and the assembly tree. You see the word in the import settings."]]],["h","What a model is made of"],["terms",[["Part","One piece of the model: a bolt, a cover, a bracket. Each part has a row in the parts tree."],["Assembly","A model made of many parts, often arranged in groups inside groups."],["Group","A folder in the parts tree. It holds parts or other groups."],["Mesh","The surface of a part, built from many small flat triangles."],["Triangle (tri)","The smallest piece of a mesh. The app counts everything in triangles: more triangles mean a heavier model."],["Polygon","Another word for the flat pieces of a mesh. In this app it means the same as triangle."],["Vertex","A corner point of a triangle. The plural is vertices."],["Normal","A hidden arrow on the surface that tells the light which way the surface faces. Wrong normals make a part look oddly shaded."],["Bounding box (bbox)","The smallest box that a part fits inside. The app uses it to measure how big a part is."],["Origin and pivot","The origin is the point 0,0,0 of the scene. A pivot is the point a part turns around."],["Material","What a surface looks like: its colour and how shiny or metallic it is. Many parts can share one material."]]],["h","What the app does to a model"],["terms",[["Tessellation","Turning the exact curved surfaces of a CAD file into triangles. Finer tessellation means smoother curves and more triangles."],["Instancing","Storing one mesh for a shape that repeats, plus the list of places where it sits. 400 identical bolts become one bolt and 400 positions."],["Decimation","Removing triangles from a mesh while keeping its overall shape. It is lossy: detail that is removed does not come back, except through undo."],["Lossy","An action that throws detail away to save size."],["Compression","Packing the same data into a smaller file. Meshopt and Draco are two methods for 3D files."],["Meshopt","A compression method for GLB files. The converter applies it through a separate tool called gltfpack, when that tool is installed."],["Draco","Another compression method for GLB files. It is a switch in the Export window."],["Scene","Everything in one tab of the app: the parts, their materials, the camera and the undo history."],["BOM","Bill of materials: a parts list. The CSV export writes one."]]],["h","The screen"],["terms",[["Viewport","The large 3D view in the middle of the window."],["Parts tree","The list of parts and groups in the left sidebar."],["Gizmo","The coloured arrows, rings or handles that appear on a selected part, for moving, rotating or scaling it."],["Orbit, pan, zoom","Turning the view around the model, sliding it sideways, and moving closer or further away."],["Isolate","Showing only the selected parts and hiding everything else for a moment."],["Draw call","One instruction to the graphics card to draw something. Fewer draw calls usually mean a smoother view. The optimisation report counts them."]]],["h","The technical side"],["terms",[["Python","A programming language. The converter and the small local server are written in it, so Python has to be installed."],["Virtual environment (.venv)","A private folder of Python add-ons that belongs to this app only. The launcher creates it inside the app folder the first time, so nothing else on your computer is changed."],["Terminal","A window where you type commands. On Windows it is called Command Prompt or PowerShell, on macOS it is called Terminal."],["Local server","A small program on your own computer that hands the app’s pages to your browser. Its address is normally `http://localhost:4242`. Localhost means this computer."],["PATH","The list of folders where your computer looks for programs. Python has to be on it for the launcher to find it."],["WebGPU","The modern way for a browser to use the graphics card. The viewer uses it when the browser has it."],["WebGL2","An older way to do the same. The viewer uses it when WebGPU is not available."],["Installing the app (PWA)","Chrome and Edge can add a web page to your desktop and Start menu as if it were a program. A page that supports this is called a progressive web app, or PWA."]]],["see",["what-it-is","file-formats","read-the-numbers"]]]},{"slug":"requirements","group":"start","title":"What you need","summary":"Windows or macOS, Python 3.10 to 3.12, a recent browser and about 2 GB of free disk space. How to check and install Python.","blocks":[["terms",[["Computer","Windows or macOS."],["Python","Version 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. Version 3.13 is not supported yet, because one of the add-ons the converter needs (cadquery-ocp) is not available for it."],["Browser","One with WebGPU: a recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. For the app to open in a window of its own, the launcher looks for Chrome, Edge or Brave."],["Disk space","About 2 GB free for the Python environment that is created on the first run."],["Internet","Needed once, on the first run, to download the Python add-ons. After that the app runs without a connection."]]],["h","Check whether you have Python"],["steps",["Open a terminal. On Windows, press the Windows key, type `cmd` and press [[Enter]]. On macOS, open the app called Terminal.","Type the command below and press [[Enter]]. On macOS, type `python3` instead of `python`.","Read the answer. `Python 3.12.7` means version 3.12 is installed, which is fine. A message that Python was not found means you need to install it."]],["code","python --version"],["note","You can skip this check. The launcher looks for Python itself and prints the version it found."],["h","Install Python on Windows"],["p","There are two ways. The first is the simplest."],["steps",["Start the app with `start.bat` (see [Start it the first time](#first-start)).","If no Python is found, the window says so and asks whether to install Python 3.12 automatically. Press [[Y]].","Wait while it installs. The launcher then carries on by itself."]],["p","Or install it yourself:"],["steps",["Go to `https://www.python.org/downloads/` and download Python 3.12.","Run the installer. On its first screen, tick “Add Python to PATH”. This is the step people miss.","Finish the installer, then close any terminal windows that were open, so they pick up the change."]],["h","Install Python on macOS"],["p","The macOS launcher uses the command `python3`. If it is missing and you have Homebrew (a tool for installing software on a Mac), the launcher installs Python through Homebrew. Without Homebrew, download Python from `https://www.python.org/downloads/` and run the launcher again. Make sure the version is 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12."],["h","Optional: gltfpack"],["p","The converter can shrink its GLB files further with Meshopt compression. It does this through a separate free tool called gltfpack, and only when gltfpack is installed and on your PATH. The app works without it. The files are simply larger."],["see",["download","first-start","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"download","group":"start","title":"Download and unzip","summary":"Get the zip, unpack it into a normal folder, and learn why there is no installer.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser comes as one zip file. There is no installer. Everything, including its Python environment, lives in the folder you unpack."],["steps",["Open the Download page of this site and press the download button. Your browser saves a zip file, usually in your Downloads folder.","Unpack the zip. On Windows, right-click it and choose the option to extract everything. On macOS, double-click it.","You now have a folder, named something like `MeshOptimiser-main`. Move it wherever you like, for example into your Documents folder.","Open the folder. You should see files called `start.bat`, `start.command`, `serve.py` and `index.html`, among others."]],["note","Unpack the zip before you start anything. Windows lets you look inside a zip without unpacking it, and the launcher does not work from there."],["h","Where to put the folder"],["list",["Somewhere you are allowed to write files. The app creates two folders inside its own: `.venv` for its Python environment and `inbox` for converted files.","On a drive with about 2 GB free."]],["p","To remove the app later, delete the folder. Nothing is installed anywhere else, apart from Python itself if you installed it for this."],["see",["first-start","files-in-folder","stop-restart-update"]]]},{"slug":"first-start","group":"start","title":"Start it the first time","summary":"Double-click the launcher for Windows or macOS. What the first run sets up, how long it takes, and what you see when it is ready.","blocks":[["p","The first start takes about three minutes, because the launcher downloads and sets up what the converter needs. Every start after that takes a second or two."],["h","On Windows"],["steps",["Open the app folder and double-click `start.bat`.","A black window with white text opens. This is the launcher. Leave it open.","It works through four numbered steps. `[1/4] Checking Python...` looks for Python and prints the version it found.","`[2/4] Checking local Python environment...` creates the folder `.venv` (about 30 seconds) and installs the add-ons (one to three minutes). You need an internet connection for this step.","`[3/4] Starting local server on http://localhost:4242` starts the small server that the app runs on.","The app opens in a window of its own and shows the start screen."]],["note","If the launcher finds no Python, it offers to install Python 3.12 for you. Press [[Y]] to accept. See [What you need](#requirements)."],["h","On macOS"],["steps",["Open the app folder and double-click `start.command`.","If macOS refuses with “Operation not permitted”, right-click `start.command` and choose “Open”. macOS blocks a double-click on a freshly unzipped script the first time.","A Terminal window opens. It creates the folder `.venv`, installs the add-ons, and prints `Starting server...`.","The app opens and shows the start screen."]],["h","What you see"],["p","The start screen has the app’s name at the top, a place to drop a file, buttons to open a file or start an empty scene, four shapes to start from, and a list of recent files, which is empty the first time."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-start.webp","The start screen: drop a file, reopen a recent one, or start from a shape."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The launcher window is the server. If you close it, the app stops working. Keep it open while you work and see [Stop, restart and update](#stop-restart-update) for the tidy way to quit.","The app opens in a window with no address bar and no browser tabs when Chrome, Edge or Brave is installed. Without one of those, it opens as a tab in your usual browser. Both work the same.","The address is normally `http://localhost:4242`. If something else on your computer already uses that number, the launcher tries another one and prints the address it chose.","It uses your usual browser profile, so your settings and recent files are remembered between starts."]],["h","Start with a file"],["p","On Windows you can drag a `.step` or `.glb` file onto `start.bat` instead of double-clicking it. The app starts and opens that file. For a STEP file the launcher window first asks a few questions, such as the tessellation quality. Press [[Enter]] at each question to accept the suggested answer."],["see",["first-file","install-as-app","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"install-as-app","group":"start","title":"Install it as an app window","summary":"Since v0.13.0 the app opens in its own window. You can also install it for a desktop shortcut, and fold its title bar away.","blocks":[["p","From v0.13.0 the launcher already opens MeshOptimiser in a window of its own: a title bar and the app, with no address bar, no browser tabs and no extension buttons. It does this when Chrome, Edge or Brave is on your computer, and you do not have to install anything for it."],["p","Installing is an extra. It gives the app its own icon, a shortcut on the desktop and in the Start menu, and a window to itself."],["h","Install it"],["steps",["Start the app as usual, so the local server is running.","Open the app in an ordinary tab of Chrome or Edge. Type the address the launcher printed, normally `http://localhost:4242`, into the browser.","The browser offers to install the page as an app (“Install”). Accept it.","The app now has a shortcut and opens in its own window."]],["note","Installing does not replace the launcher. The installed app is a window onto the same local server, so start the server first with `start.bat` or `start.command`. Without it the window has nothing to show."],["h","Fold the title bar away"],["p","In the installed app, the title bar has a small `^` button. Press it and the title bar disappears. The window’s own buttons (minimise, maximise, close) float over the top right corner, and the app’s top bar becomes the title bar."],["list",["Drag an empty stretch of the top bar to move the window.","Buttons, tabs and fields in the top bar still work as before.","Press `^` again to bring the title bar back."]],["h","An ordinary browser tab instead"],["p","If you prefer the app in a normal tab, start the server from a terminal in the app folder with the option `--tab`. [The command-line converter](#command-line) explains how to open a terminal in the folder."],["code","python serve.py --tab"],["h","Start without the black window (Windows)"],["p","The file `start_hidden.vbs` starts the app the same way as `start.bat`, without showing the launcher window. Use `start.bat` when you want to see what the launcher is doing, for example when something does not start."],["see",["first-start","stop-restart-update","the-window"]]]},{"slug":"first-file","group":"start","title":"Open your first file","summary":"Drop a file on the window. What the import settings mean, what conversion does, how to follow its progress, and where the result is kept.","blocks":[["p","The app opens STEP (`.step`, `.stp`), GLB, glTF, FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files. A STEP file is converted first. The other formats open directly."],["h","Open a file"],["steps",["Drag the file from your file manager onto the app window. You can drop it on the start screen or on the 3D view. While you hold it over the view, the words {{Drop to open}} appear.","Or press [[Ctrl + O]] and pick the file. On the start screen the same button is called {{Open file…}}.","For a STEP file, a window called {{Import settings}} appears. The first time, leave everything as it is and press {{Import}}.","A progress card shows what is happening, with a running clock and a log of messages. A large assembly can take several minutes.","When it is done, the model appears in the 3D view. The parts tree on the left fills with its parts, and {{Properties}} on the right shows its totals."]],["note","Your original file is never changed. The app works on its own copy, and nothing is written to a file until you save or export."],["h","What conversion does"],["p","A STEP file holds exact surfaces. The converter, running in Python on your computer, turns it into a GLB file made of triangles. Along the way it:"],["list",["reads the names and colours of the parts and the tree they are arranged in;","tessellates the surfaces, which means it covers them with triangles;","finds parts that have the same shape and stores that shape once (instancing);","leaves out parts below a size you choose, if you ask for it;","compresses the result with Meshopt, if the tool gltfpack is installed."]],["h","The import settings for a STEP file"],["terms",[["Tessellation quality","How closely the triangles follow curved surfaces. The choices run from Coarse (1.0), the fastest with visibly faceted curves, to Ultra (0.05), slow and very smooth. Standard (0.5) is the recommended one."],["Colours & materials","Read colours, names and the hierarchy, or geometry only. Geometry only is the fastest and gives you neither colours nor a tree. It suits huge files where you only need the shape."],["XCAF read modes","Five switches for extra information in the file. Each one costs time. Turning off {{SHUO overrides}} saves the most on assemblies with many repeated parts."],["Drop parts smaller than (% of model)","Leaves out tiny parts during conversion. 0 keeps everything."],["Force re-convert","Ignores a stored conversion of the same file, if there is one, and converts from scratch."],["Collapse identical shapes into instances","Stores a repeated shape once. Leave it on."],["Append to current scene","Adds the file to the scene that is open, instead of opening it as a scene of its own."],["Fit view after loading","Moves the camera so the whole model is in view when it has loaded."],["Don't ask again for this format","Skips this window next time and uses the same answers. Leave it unticked until you know which settings you want."]]],["p","FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files get a shorter version of this window, with only the last three choices. GLB and glTF files open without it."],["h","Follow the progress, or stop it"],["list",["The progress card shows the latest message from the converter. {{Copy log}} copies all messages, which is useful when you report a problem.","{{Cancel}} stops the conversion.","If the conversion fails, the card says so. Press {{Copy log}} to keep the messages. See [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)."]],["h","Where the converted file goes"],["p","The converter writes the GLB into the folder `inbox`, inside the app folder. The copy of your STEP file that was made for the conversion is removed when it finishes. To keep a GLB somewhere you choose, with a name you choose, use [Save a scene](#save-scene) or [Export](#export)."],["h","More than one file"],["list",["Opening a second file does not replace the first. It opens in a new tab. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs).","To put two files into one scene, use {{Menu}}, then {{Import into scene…}}, or press [[Ctrl + Shift + O]].","To throw away every edit and load the file again as it was, use {{Menu}}, then {{Revert to source…}}."]],["see",["the-window","move-the-view","read-the-numbers","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"stop-restart-update","group":"start","title":"Stop, restart and update","summary":"Quit the app cleanly, start it again in a second or two, and move to a newer version without losing your settings.","blocks":[["h","Stop the app"],["steps",["Click {{Menu}} in the top bar and choose {{Quit app}}.","If the scene has parts in it, the app asks whether to save first. Choose to save, to quit without saving, or to cancel.","The page says that MeshOptimiser is closed and the server has stopped. On Windows the launcher window closes by itself.","Close the app window if it is still open."]],["p","Closing only the app window does not stop the server. The launcher window stays open and the server keeps running. You can also stop it there: click the launcher window and press [[Ctrl + C]], or close that window."],["h","Start it again"],["p","Double-click `start.bat` or `start.command` again. The launcher finds the `.venv` folder from the first run and reuses it, so the app is ready in a second or two."],["p","With the start screen’s resume option on, the file you had open last is offered at the top of the start screen. See [Settings](#settings)."],["h","Update to a new version"],["p","There is no updater. A new version is a new zip. This is a suggested way to move over, based on how the app stores things:"],["steps",["Quit the app.","Download the new zip and unpack it into a new folder, next to the old one.","If you want to keep converted files, copy them out of the old folder’s `inbox` folder.","Start the new version with its launcher. The first start sets up its Python environment again and takes about three minutes.","When you are happy with the new version, delete the old folder."]],["note","Your settings and the recent files list are kept by your browser, not in the app folder, so they are still there after an update."],["see",["first-start","files-in-folder","save-scene"]]]},{"slug":"cad-files-step-first","group":"cad","title":"Native CAD files: export a STEP file first","summary":"MeshOptimiser opens STEP, not SolidWorks, Inventor, Creo or CATIA files. Why, which STEP version to pick, and where to find the steps for your program.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser reads STEP files. It does not read the files your CAD program saves for itself, such as SolidWorks (`.sldprt`, `.sldasm`), Inventor (`.ipt`, `.iam`), Creo (`.prt`, `.asm`), Fusion (`.f3d`), Rhino (`.3dm`), CATIA or NX files. Those formats belong to the company that made the program, and reading them needs that company’s own software, which a free app cannot include."],["p","Almost every CAD program can write STEP, and STEP is the one format they all share. So the job is always the same: open your model in your CAD program, save a copy as STEP, and open that copy in MeshOptimiser."],["h","Pick your program"],["list",["[Autodesk Inventor](#step-from-inventor)","[Onshape](#step-from-onshape)","[PTC Creo](#step-from-creo)","[Rhino](#step-from-rhino)"]],["p","Using something else, such as NX, CATIA or Solid Edge? Look for Export or Save As in its File menu and choose STEP. The ideas below apply to any program."],["h","What a STEP file keeps, and what it leaves behind"],["terms",[["Kept","The exact shape of every part, the assembly tree with the names of the parts, and the colours, if the STEP version you choose carries them."],["Left behind","The feature tree, sketches, dimensions and the history of how the part was made."]]],["h","Which STEP version to choose"],["p","Many programs ask for an “application protocol”, a short code for how much information goes into the file."],["terms",[["AP203","The plain one: geometry, the structure and configuration data. No colours and no layers."],["AP214","Everything AP203 has, plus colours and layers. This is the usual choice for MeshOptimiser, because the colours arrive."],["AP242","The newest. It merges AP203 and AP214 and adds more manufacturing information. Not every program offers it, and some do not offer it for assemblies."]]],["p","If your program offers AP214, choose it. If it does not, use whatever it calls its normal STEP export and check the colours after opening the file."],["h","Export the whole assembly into one file"],["p","MeshOptimiser opens one file. Several programs can also write an assembly as a top-level file plus one file per part. Leave that switched off, so that everything is in one STEP file."],["h","If you cannot get a STEP file"],["p","Export a mesh instead, as OBJ, FBX, STL or GLB. MeshOptimiser opens those directly. You lose something: the copies of a repeated part are found when a STEP file is converted, so a mesh file arrives with every copy as a mesh of its own, and the model is heavier to start with."],["note","Menu names change a little from one version of a CAD program to the next. If your screen differs from these steps, search your program’s own help for “export STEP”."],["see",["first-file","file-formats","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-inventor","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP assembly from Autodesk Inventor","summary":"Save an Inventor assembly as one STEP file with Save Copy As, pick AP214, and learn what Shrinkwrap can do for a very large assembly.","blocks":[["p","Inventor writes STEP with Save Copy As. When you save an assembly this way, the assembly and all the parts it refers to go into a single file, which is what MeshOptimiser wants."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the assembly (the `.iam` file).","Choose {{File}}, {{Save As}}, then {{Save Copy As}}.","In {{Save as type}}, choose the STEP files entry (`*.stp`, `*.ste`, `*.step`).","Click {{Options}}. The STEP options window opens.","Under the application protocol, choose {{214}}.","Leave the spline fit accuracy as it is, press {{OK}}, name the file and press {{Save}}."]],["h","What the choices mean"],["terms",[["203","Configuration Controlled Design. The plain protocol, without colours."],["214","Automotive Design. The one to choose here, because it also carries colours and layers."],["242","Managed Model Based 3D Engineering. It includes product manufacturing information. Inventor does not offer it for assemblies."],["Spline fit accuracy","How closely curves are approximated, between 0.00001 cm and 0.001 cm. A smaller value is more exact and makes a bigger file. The default is fine."]]],["h","If the assembly is very large"],["p","Inventor has a command called Shrinkwrap that builds a single simplified part from an assembly. One of its options, {{Remove internal parts}}, is on by default. It looks at the model from 14 directions and drops the parts that cannot be seen, which is close to what {{Select hidden parts}} does in MeshOptimiser. You can export the shrinkwrapped part as STEP instead of the full assembly. Autodesk’s help describes the command under “Create a Shrinkwrap part”."],["p","You do not have to do this. MeshOptimiser finds the hidden parts itself, so the full assembly works too. Shrinkwrap is useful when the full assembly is too big for your computer to export or convert comfortably, or when you want a simplified part for other reasons."],["note","If your version of Inventor looks different, search Autodesk’s help for “Save As Export Options”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file). To take out the hidden parts there, see [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","select-hidden-parts","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-onshape","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP assembly from Onshape","summary":"Export an Onshape assembly or Part Studio as STEP by right-clicking its tab, and why STEP is a better start than Onshape’s own glTF export.","blocks":[["p","Onshape works in the browser, and so does its export. You export from the tab at the bottom of the window."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open your model. For a whole assembly, open the Assembly tab. For parts, open the Part Studio.","Right-click the tab at the bottom of the window and choose {{Export}}.","Choose {{STEP}} as the format.","Check the options (below). The defaults are fine for a first try.","Choose how you want the file: {{Download}} saves it to your computer.","Press {{Export}}."]],["p","In an assembly, the tab’s Export writes the whole assembly at once."],["h","The options that matter"],["terms",[["Use latest version","On by default. The latest supported version of STEP is written."],["Use custom units for export","STEP only. Choose Millimeter, Centimeter, Meter, Inch or Foot. Millimetres are the usual choice for CAD."],["Preprocessing","STEP only. Options to remove small entities or to convert all surfaces to b-surfaces before export. Leave it as it is."]]],["h","Why STEP and not glTF"],["p","Onshape can also export glTF and GLB directly. People on Onshape’s own forum report that this export writes a separate mesh for every face of a part, which is slow to draw in real-time viewers. Exporting STEP and letting MeshOptimiser build the mesh avoids that, and it lets MeshOptimiser find the copies of repeated parts."],["note","If Onshape’s dialog looks different in your version, search its help for “Exporting files”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","first-file","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-creo","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP assembly from PTC Creo","summary":"Save a Creo assembly as one STEP file with Save a Copy, set the application protocol and keep Export assembly as on Single File.","blocks":[["p","Creo exports STEP with Save a Copy. The settings live in a window called STEP Export Profile Settings."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the assembly.","Choose {{File}}, {{Save As}}, then {{Save a Copy}}.","In the {{Type}} list, choose {{STEP (*.stp)}}.","Enter a file name, or accept the default.","Click {{Options}}. The editor {{STEP Export Profile Settings}} opens.","Set {{Application protocol}} to {{ap214_is}}.","Check that {{Export assembly as}} is on {{Single File}}. That is the default.","Press {{OK}} in the editor, and {{OK}} in the Save a Copy window."]],["h","What the choices mean"],["terms",[["Application protocol","Creo lists ap202_is, ap203_is (the default), ap203_is_ext, ap203_e2, ap209_dis, ap214_is, ap242 and ap242_e3. ap214_is is the AP214 protocol, the one that adds colours and layers in other CAD programs. Open the result in MeshOptimiser and check that the colours came through."],["Export assembly as: Single File","Writes all the geometry of the assembly into one file. This is what MeshOptimiser needs."],["Export assembly as: Separate Parts","Writes the structure to one file and every part as a file of its own. Do not use it for this."],["Export assembly as: Separate All","Writes the assembly, every sub-assembly and every part as files of their own. Do not use it for this."]]],["p","The {{Export assembly as}} choice appears when the application protocol is ap214_is, ap203_e2, ap242 or ap242_e3. PTC’s help says Separate Parts and Separate All are offered only for those protocols."],["h","Other things in the export"],["list",["Tick {{Customize Export}} in the Save a Copy window if you want to choose which layers go in, or the coordinate system. The defaults are fine to begin with.","The profile can be saved and loaded again with {{Load Profile}}, so you set it up once."]],["h","If the assembly is very large"],["p","Work on a copy, remove what you will not show, such as hardware inside housings, and export that. A smaller file converts faster. See also [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) for ways to speed up the conversion."],["note","Menus differ between Creo releases. If yours does, search PTC’s help for “Exporting Part or Assembly to STEP”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","first-file","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-rhino","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP file from Rhino","summary":"Export selected Rhino objects as STEP with File, Export Selected: which protocol keeps colours, what happens to black objects, and what to do with meshes.","blocks":[["p","Rhino models are made of surfaces and solids, and Rhino writes them to STEP with its Export command."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the objects you want to export.","Choose {{File}}, then {{Export Selected}}. {{Save As}} writes the whole model instead.","In {{Save as type}}, choose {{STEP (.stp; .step)}}.","Name the file and press {{Save}}.","The window {{STEP Export Options}} opens. Choose {{AP214AutomotiveDesign}} as the schema, so colours and layers come along.","Leave the other options as they are, and press {{OK}}."]],["h","What the choices mean"],["terms",[["AP203ConfigControlDesign","The default. Geometry, topology and configuration data, without colours and layers."],["AP214AutomotiveDesign","Everything AP203 has, plus colours, layers and GD&T. Choose this one."],["AP214AutomotiveDesignCC2","A minor syntax update of the earlier AP214."],["AP242","The newest standard, new in Rhino 8. It merges AP203 and AP214."]]],["p","Rhino’s help says your company’s standards decide which schema to use, and that without a standard the default is fine. For MeshOptimiser, AP214AutomotiveDesign is the one that keeps colours."],["h","Things to know"],["list",["A Rhino object that is black gets no colour in the STEP file, so the next program gives it its default colour. This is on purpose, because black objects look like ink blots in some programs. If you want a colour on them, recolour the parts in MeshOptimiser.","SubD objects are turned into polysurfaces when they are exported.","If your model is already a mesh, for example from a scan, STEP is not the best route. Export it as OBJ, FBX or STL instead. MeshOptimiser opens those directly."]],["note","If Rhino’s dialog looks different in your version, search the Rhino help for “STEP import and export”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","first-file","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"the-window","group":"around","title":"The window","summary":"A tour of the screen: the top bar and tabs, the parts tree, the 3D view and its toolbars, the cards on the right and the status bar.","blocks":[["p","The window has four areas: a top bar, a left sidebar, the 3D view in the middle (called the viewport) and a right sidebar. A thin status bar runs under the viewport."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-viewer.webp","The whole window with an assembly open."],["h","Top bar"],["terms",[["The app’s mark","At the far left. Click it for the version number, what is new, a link to report a problem and the list of keyboard shortcuts."],["Sidebar button","Shows or hides the left sidebar."],["Menu","The file commands: {{New scene}}, {{Open file…}}, {{Import into scene…}}, {{Save scene…}}, {{Export…}}, {{Revert to source…}}, {{Scene settings…}}, {{Settings…}}, {{Welcome screen}} and {{Quit app}}."],["Parts / Library switch","Two small buttons that choose what the left sidebar shows: the parts of the scene, or the library of parts you can add."],["Scene tabs","One tab for each open scene, and a `+` to start a new one. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["Export","At the right. Writes the model to a file for another program. See [Export](#export)."],["Camera button","Next to Export. Saves a picture of the view. See [Screenshots](#screenshots)."]]],["h","Left sidebar"],["p","It shows the parts tree: every part of the model, arranged in groups, with a search field and four buttons above it ({{Locate}}, {{Collapse}}, {{Rename}}, {{Flatten}}). Switch it to the library to add shapes and standard parts. A {{Transform}} panel with position, rotation and size can be shown at its foot."],["h","Viewport"],["terms",[["Tool strip, left edge","Move, rotate and scale tools, a button for the transform panel, then the five view modes (solid, wireframe, X-ray, heatmap, clay) and a button that highlights small parts."],["View name, top centre","Says which camera view is active, {{Cam}} to begin with. Click it to switch to Top, Front or Side. When parts are isolated, a second pill next to it says so."],["View cube, top right","A small cube that turns with the model. Click a face to look straight at that side."],["Toolbar, bottom centre","Search, add a shape, {{Split}}, {{Fill holes}}, {{Smart fit}}, {{Select hidden parts}}, {{Measure}}, {{Materials}}, and `…` for the list of all commands."],["Shortcut tips, bottom right","A faint list of what the mouse and keys do right now. It changes when you select something."],["Command panels, bottom left","A tool with settings, such as Fill holes, opens as a panel here. [[Enter]] runs it and [[Esc]] puts it away."]]],["h","Right sidebar"],["p","At the top is {{Properties}}. With nothing selected it describes the whole scene. With a selection it describes that. Under it are cards you open and close by clicking their title:"],["terms",[["Selection & actions","Select all, invert, select similar, by colour, by name, and Merge, Group and Delete."],["Inspect","{{Heavy parts}}, {{Filters & flags}}, {{Measurements}} and {{Exploded view}}."],["Clean up","{{Delete small parts}}, {{Auto cleanup}}, {{Align to floor}} and {{Tidy geometry}}."],["Reduce","{{Reduce triangles}}, which holds Fit to budget, Decimate, Smart fit all parts and the optimisation report."]]],["h","Status bar"],["p","Under the viewport: the number of parts, triangles and vertices, the size of the mesh data, how many parts are selected (click it to frame them), how many are flagged (click it to isolate them), the frame rate, and a {{Console}} button."],["h","Console and Materials"],["p","Two panels slide up from the bottom and take turns. The console, opened with the backtick key or the {{Console}} button in the status bar, is a log of everything the app has done, with filters for information, warnings and errors and a button to copy it. The materials dock ([[Shift + M]]) lists every material in the scene. See [Colours and materials](#colours-materials)."],["h","Make room"],["list",["Drag the inner edge of either sidebar to make it wider or narrower.","Use the sidebar button in the top bar to hide the left sidebar.","On a narrow window, buttons of the bottom toolbar fold away into the `…` list."]],["see",["move-the-view","parts-tree","search-and-commands","read-the-numbers"]]]},{"slug":"move-the-view","group":"around","title":"Move the view","summary":"Orbit, pan and zoom with the mouse, frame a part with F, and switch between the camera, Top, Front and Side views.","blocks":[["p","Moving the view never changes the model. You are only moving the camera."],["h","With the mouse"],["keys",[["Drag","Orbit: turn the view around the model (left mouse button)"],["Middle drag","Pan: slide the view sideways (press the wheel and drag)"],["Right drag","Pan as well"],["Scroll","Zoom in and out"],["Alt + Right drag","Zoom smoothly: drag up to come closer"]]],["h","Bring things into view"],["keys",[["F","Frame: fit the selected parts in the view, or the whole model when nothing is selected"]]],["p","If you get lost, click an empty spot to clear the selection and press [[F]]. The whole model comes back into view."],["h","Standard views"],["p","The pill at the top centre of the viewport names the current view. Click it to choose another."],["keys",[["Ctrl + 1","Cam: the free camera with perspective"],["Ctrl + 2","Top"],["Ctrl + 3","Front"],["Ctrl + 4","Side"]]],["p","Top, Front and Side are flat views without perspective, like a technical drawing. As soon as you orbit out of one, the app returns to the free camera from where you are."],["h","The view cube"],["p","The small cube in the top right corner turns with the model. Click one of its faces, or the dot at the end of an axis, to look straight along that axis: Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Side or Left. The view pill shows the name."],["h","The floor grid"],["p","The grid marks the floor of the scene. Press [[G]] to hide or show it."],["h","Settings that change how the view moves"],["p","Under {{Settings}}, {{Camera}}:"],["terms",[["Projection","Perspective, where far things look smaller, or Orthographic, where they do not."],["Field of view","How wide the lens is."],["Clip planes","How near and how far the camera can see. Leave it on Auto unless parts of a very small or very large model are cut off."],["Orbit around the selection","With something selected, the view turns around it. Off, or with nothing selected, it turns around the centre of the view."],["Zoom toward the cursor","The wheel zooms toward the pointer instead of the centre."]]],["note","On a heavy model, very small parts are left out while the view is moving and drawn again the moment it stops. This keeps movement smooth. You can turn it off under {{Settings}}, {{Performance}}, {{Skip tiny parts}}."],["see",["select-parts","view-modes","settings","shortcuts"]]]},{"slug":"select-parts","group":"around","title":"Select parts","summary":"Click, add with Shift, toggle with Ctrl, drag a box, or let the app select by shape, colour or name.","blocks":[["p","Most tools work on the parts you have selected. A selected part gets an outline in the viewport and its row is highlighted in the parts tree."],["h","In the viewport"],["keys",[["Click","Select one part. The tree scrolls to its row."],["Shift + Click","Add a part to the selection"],["Ctrl + Click","Add a part, or take it out if it was selected"],["Ctrl + Drag","Box select: draw a rectangle around parts"],["Ctrl + Shift + Drag","Box select and add to what is already selected"],["Esc","Select nothing. A click on empty space does the same."]]],["h","In the parts tree"],["keys",[["Click","Select one part. Click a group to select every part inside it."],["Ctrl + Click","Add or remove one row"],["Shift + Click","Select every row between the last one you clicked and this one"]]],["h","Let the app select"],["p","The card {{Selection & actions}} in the right sidebar has buttons for the common cases:"],["terms",[["All","Selects every visible part. [[Ctrl + A]]."],["Invert","Selects what was not selected, and the other way round. [[Ctrl + I]]."],["Clear","Selects nothing. [[Esc]]."],["Select similar","Adds every part that has the same shape as the selection. Select one bolt, press it, and all the bolts like it are selected."],["By colour","Selects every part that shares a colour with the selection."],["By name","Asks for a pattern and selects every part whose name matches it. Typing `screw` selects every part with screw in its name, in capitals or not. The pattern is a regular expression, a compact way to describe text, so some characters such as `.` and `*` have a special meaning."]]],["p","Two tools select for you in a cleverer way: [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts) and the size threshold of [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["h","Find what you selected"],["list",["Press [[Shift + S]], or the {{Locate}} button above the tree, to scroll the tree to the selected part and open the groups it is in.","Press [[F]] to frame the selection in the viewport.","The status bar shows how many parts are selected. Click that number to frame them.","{{Properties}} shows the triangle count of the selection and its share of the scene."]],["h","The right-click menu"],["p","Right-click a part, in the viewport or in the tree, for a menu of things to do with the selection: frame it, isolate it, select similar parts, copy, duplicate or delete."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-context-menu.webp","Right-click a part to frame, isolate, select similar, copy or delete."],["note","A common mistake: dragging without [[Ctrl]] turns the view instead of drawing a selection box. Hold [[Ctrl]] first, then drag."],["see",["hide-isolate","parts-tree","search-and-commands"]]]},{"slug":"hide-isolate","group":"around","title":"Hide, isolate, show all","summary":"Hide parts that are in the way, look at a few parts on their own, and bring everything back with one key.","blocks":[["p","Hiding is how you look inside a model. A hidden part is still in the scene. It is only not drawn."],["keys",[["H","Hide the selected parts"],["Shift + H","Hide the rest: everything that is not selected"],["Alt + H","Show all parts again"],["S","Isolate the selection. Press it again to show everything."]]],["h","Isolate"],["steps",["Select the parts you want to look at.","Press [[S]]. Everything else disappears.","A pill at the top of the viewport says so, for example “Isolated · 1 of 43 parts”.","Press [[S]] again, or click the pill, to show everything."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-isolate.webp","A set of parts on their own, isolated."],["h","In the parts tree"],["p","Every row has an eye at its right end. Click it to hide or show that part. The eye on a group hides or shows everything inside it."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["Hiding and showing are undo steps. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes one back.","{{Properties}} shows how many parts are hidden.","A saved scene remembers which parts were hidden.","When you export, the switch {{Visible parts only}} decides whether hidden parts are written to the file. See [Export](#export)."]],["note","Hiding a part does not make the file lighter. To remove a part for good, delete it with [[Del]]."],["see",["select-parts","view-modes","select-hidden-parts"]]]},{"slug":"parts-tree","group":"around","title":"The parts tree","summary":"Search, rename, group and reorder the parts of a model, rename many at once, and flatten a hierarchy that is deeper than you need.","blocks":[["p","The parts tree in the left sidebar lists everything in the scene. A part is one row. A group is a folder that holds parts or other groups. A STEP file usually arrives with the groups its designer made."],["h","Read a row"],["list",["A part row shows its name and its triangle count.","A group row shows how many parts it holds. A group with nothing in it says “empty”.","The eye at the right hides or shows the row.","Click the small box in front of a group to open or close it."]],["h","The four buttons"],["terms",[["Locate","Scrolls the tree to the selected part and opens the groups it is in. [[Shift + S]]."],["Collapse","Closes every group. Press it again to open them all."],["Rename","Renames many parts at once. [[F2]]."],["Flatten","Removes levels of grouping."]]],["h","Search"],["p","Type in {{Search parts…}} above the tree. Only the rows whose names match stay, including matches inside closed groups. Press the small cross in the field to clear the search."],["h","Rename one row"],["steps",["Double-click the name of a part or a group.","Type the new name.","Press [[Enter]]."]],["h","Rename many rows"],["p","CAD files often come with names like `Part_0031_geom`. Batch rename changes many names in one go."],["steps",["Select the parts to rename.","Press [[F2]] or the {{Rename}} button. The {{Batch rename}} window opens.","Choose a tab. {{Find & Replace}} swaps one piece of text for another. {{Pattern}} builds each name from pieces such as `{name}`, `{parent}` and `{counter:003}`. {{Presets}} has ready-made rules, such as lower-casing every name or removing a number at the end.","Look at the {{Preview}}. It shows every name before and after.","Press {{Apply}}."]],["h","Group and ungroup"],["keys",[["Ctrl + G","Put the selected parts in a new group"],["Ctrl + Shift + G","Ungroup: remove the selected group and keep what was inside"],["Del","Delete the selected parts. With a group selected, the group and everything in it."]]],["p","A new group is called “Group” with a number. Double-click it to give it a name. Parts in a group move together when you move the group."],["h","Drag and drop"],["p","Drag a row onto a group to move it into that group, or between two rows to change the order. A group cannot be dropped into itself."],["h","Flatten"],["p","A STEP file can have groups inside groups ten levels deep. Flatten removes levels you do not need. Press {{Flatten}} to open the {{Advanced flatten}} window."],["terms",[["Scope","The whole tree, or only the groups you selected."],["Total flatten","Removes every group inside the scope. All parts end up side by side."],["Keep top N levels","Keeps the first levels of grouping and removes everything deeper."],["Flatten last level only","Removes only the deepest groups, the ones that hold nothing but parts."],["Collapse single-child chains","Removes groups that hold nothing but one other group. This is a common tidy-up for STEP files."],["Ungroup (dissolve scope itself)","Removes the selected groups and moves what they held one level up."],["Preserve user groups","Leaves alone the groups you made yourself."],["Remove empty groups left behind","Deletes groups that end up with nothing in them."]]],["h","The right-click menu"],["p","Right-click a part row for frame, isolate, hide, select similar, group, merge, Split, Fill holes, lock, Smart fit, copy, duplicate and delete. Right-click a group row to select its parts, frame it, rename it, ungroup it or delete it with its contents."],["p","A locked part cannot be moved, and delete skips it. Use the same menu entry to unlock it."],["h","Undo"],["p","Every change to the tree is an undo step: renames, groups, drags, flatten and deletes. Press [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["note","By default an emptied group stays in the tree, marked “empty”, so you can fill it again. To have such groups removed automatically, switch on {{Delete groups when they become empty}} under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["see",["select-parts","undo-redo","cleanup"]]]},{"slug":"organise-the-tree","group":"around","title":"Organise the tree: groups, sorting and picking","summary":"Pick groups and parts together, take a group out of the tree without losing what is in it, flatten, sort and move groups.","blocks":[["p","The parts tree can be tidied from the right-click menu of a group, and groups and parts can be picked together. Everything here is one undo step."],["h","Pick groups and parts together"],["keys",[["Click","Picks this row alone. A group brings all its parts."],["Ctrl + Click","Adds the row to the selection, or takes it out again. A group goes in or out with all its parts."],["Shift + Click","Picks every row from the last one you clicked to this one, groups and parts alike. Only the rows you can see count: a closed group comes with its group, not row by row."],["Ctrl + Shift + Click","Adds that range to what is already picked."]]],["p","Control and Shift never start a drag, so a slip of the mouse cannot move rows when you only meant to pick them."],["h","The right-click menu of a group"],["terms",[["Ungroup","Dissolves this group; its children move up one level."],["Remove group, keep contents","Takes the group, or every selected group when it is one of them, out of the tree. What was inside stays as it was, nesting included, and moves up to where the group stood."],["Flatten this group","Keeps the group but dissolves every group inside it, so all its parts sit directly under it."],["Sort inside A to Z and Z to A","Orders what is directly inside the group by name. Numbers go by value (part 2 before part 10) and groups come first."],["Move group up and Move group down","Moves the group one place among the rows beside it, with its contents."],["Delete empty groups","Removes every group that holds no part."],["Expand this branch and Collapse this branch","Opens or closes this group and every group inside it."],["Delete group + contents","Deletes the group and everything in it. Ctrl + Z brings it back."]]],["note","Lines you draw are rows of the tree too, so they can be grouped, nested, sorted and moved in the same way."],["see",["parts-tree","select-parts","undo-redo","draw-lines"]]]},{"slug":"search-and-commands","group":"around","title":"Search and commands","summary":"Press Ctrl + K to find any command, part or library part by name, and to work out a sum or convert a length.","blocks":[["p","You do not have to remember where a button is. The search finds it. It is the fastest way to use the app once you know roughly what you want."],["h","Open the search"],["steps",["Press [[Ctrl + K]], or click the magnifying glass at the left of the bottom toolbar.","Start typing. The list below narrows as you type.","Use [[↑]] and [[↓]] to choose a line.","Press [[Enter]] to run it. Press [[Esc]] to close the search without doing anything."]],["h","What it finds"],["terms",[["Commands","Everything in the menus and on the toolbars, each with its shortcut. Before you type, the commands you ran lately come first, with suggestions that fit what is selected."],["Sidebar controls","Every button, slider, switch and field in the sidebars, listed under the name of its card. Choosing a button runs it. Choosing anything else opens its card and scrolls to it."],["Parts","Parts of the scene by name. [[Enter]] selects the part and frames it, even when it is hidden."],["Library parts","Parts you can add. Type `add flange` or just `flange`, and [[Enter]] adds one."]]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-search.webp","Search for “bolt”: the parts in the scene by name, and the bolts in the library ready to add."],["h","A calculator"],["p","Type a sum or a length and the search works it out. [[Enter]] copies the answer, ready to paste into a field."],["terms",[["12*25.4","A sum."],["2 in","A length in inches, given in millimetres."],["50 mm in in","A length converted to the unit you name."],["3 thou","Thousandths of an inch, given in millimetres."]]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-search-sum.webp","The search works out a sum or a length. Enter copies the answer."],["h","The list of all commands"],["p","The `…` button at the right end of the bottom toolbar opens every command that acts on the model in one list, with its shortcut. A command that cannot run right now is dimmed. Hover over it to see why, for example because nothing is selected."],["h","Command panels"],["p","Some commands have settings: Split, Fill holes, Smart fit and Select hidden parts. They open as a panel at the bottom left of the viewport."],["keys",[["Enter","Run the open panel"],["Esc","Put the panel away. A second press clears the selection."]]],["h","Numbers you can drag"],["p","Any number field in the app can be dragged sideways to change it, or clicked to type a value. Hold [[Shift]] while dragging to go ten times faster and [[Alt]] to go ten times finer."],["see",["shortcuts","library","the-window"]]]},{"slug":"undo-redo","group":"around","title":"Undo and redo","summary":"Every editing action can be taken back with Ctrl + Z. What counts as one step, and how to go back to the file as it was opened.","blocks":[["keys",[["Ctrl + Z","Undo the last action"],["Ctrl + Y","Redo: do it again"],["Ctrl + Shift + Z","Redo as well"]]],["p","There are no undo buttons on screen. Undo and redo are keyboard only."],["h","What can be undone"],["p","Every editing action: deleting, hiding and showing, moving, renaming, grouping, flattening, recolouring, and every mesh tool such as Fill holes, Decimate, Smart fit and Split."],["h","What counts as one step"],["list",["A tool that changes many parts at once is one step. One [[Ctrl + Z]] takes back a whole Fill holes run or a whole Fit to budget.","In Align to floor, a run of adjustments is one step.","Changing the threshold of Delete small parts while its parts are isolated adds no step of its own."]],["h","Each tab has its own history"],["p","Undo works on the scene in the tab you are looking at. Other tabs are not touched. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["h","Start over"],["p","To throw away every edit and reload the file as it was, choose {{Menu}}, then {{Revert to source…}}. The app asks before it does this."],["note","Your original file on disk is never changed by anything you do in the app. Edits reach a file only when you save a scene or export."],["note","After a change that is too large to keep an undo copy of, the app clears the history and tells you so. This is rare. Save a scene before a very heavy operation if you want a way back."],["see",["save-scene","scene-tabs","shortcuts"]]]},{"slug":"scene-tabs","group":"around","title":"Scene tabs","summary":"Each tab is a scene of its own. Open several files side by side, drag tabs into order, and save from the tab’s icon.","blocks":[["p","A scene is everything you are working on in one tab: its parts, materials, camera and undo history. The tabs sit in the top bar, like tabs in a browser. Each one is a separate scene."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-tabs.webp","Scene tabs: drag them into order. The dot on the icon is yellow while there are unsaved changes."],["h","Open and close"],["list",["Click the `+` after the last tab, or press [[Ctrl + N]], for a new empty scene.","Opening a file never replaces a scene that has something in it. The file opens in a new tab.","Click a tab to switch to it. Nothing is reloaded.","Click the cross at the right end of a tab to close it. A click with the middle mouse button closes it too.","If the scene has changes that are not saved, the app asks before it closes the tab."]],["h","Read a tab"],["terms",[["Name","The name of the scene. Double-click it to rename the scene."],["Dot on the icon","Yellow means there are changes that are not saved. Green means the scene is saved."],["Icon","Click it to save the scene. It does the same as [[Ctrl + S]]."]]],["h","Put tabs in order"],["steps",["Press a tab and pull it sideways.","The tab rides with the pointer and the others slide over to make room.","Let go. It drops into the nearest place."]],["p","Press [[Esc]] while you are still holding the tab to put it back where it was."],["h","Sidebars across tabs"],["p","When you switch tabs, the widths of the sidebars and the folded left sidebar stay as they were in the tab you came from. To let each tab keep its own layout, switch off {{Same sidebars in every tab}} under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["see",["save-scene","first-file","undo-redo"]]]},{"slug":"settings","group":"around","title":"Settings","summary":"One window with six sections and a search. What each section holds, in a sentence or two.","blocks":[["p","Open it with [[Ctrl + ,]] or {{Menu}}, then {{Settings…}}. Changes apply as you make them. There is nothing to confirm: press {{Done}} or [[Esc]] to close the window."],["p","The field at the top searches every section at once. Press [[Ctrl + F]] to jump to it."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-settings-general.webp","Settings, General."],["h","General"],["terms",[["Start screen","Show the start screen when the app opens."],["Offer the last file","A Resume button on the start screen for the file you had open last."],["Fit the view after loading","Move the camera so a newly opened model fills the view."],["Same sidebars in every tab","Keep the sidebar widths when you switch tabs."],["Confirm destructive actions","Ask before deletes and other steps that are hard to take back."],["Delete groups when they become empty","Remove a group automatically when an action empties it. Off by default."]]],["h","Viewport"],["p","What the 3D view draws around the model."],["terms",[["Highlight small parts","Tints the parts that are under the size threshold of Delete small parts."],["Ground grid","The floor grid. [[G]] switches it too."],["Sun direction gizmo","A handle in the viewport for turning the light."],["FPS counter and Renderer stats","Small read-outs of how fast the view is drawing."],["Group origins on select","Shows the dot at a group’s origin only while the group is selected."],["Fog","Fades the grid and distant parts into the background, with start, end and intensity."],["Background","Dark, Studio gradient, Neutral grey, White, or HDRI environment. An HDRI is a picture of surroundings that lights the model. There are four built-in ones (Studio, Outdoor, Sunset, Overcast), or load your own `.hdr` or `.exr` file."]]],["h","Camera"],["p","Projection, field of view, clip planes, and two navigation switches. They are explained in [Move the view](#move-the-view)."],["h","Performance"],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-settings-performance.webp","Settings, Performance: the renderer, quality, and what a moving view gives up."],["terms",[["Renderer","WebGPU, or WebGL2 where the browser or the graphics driver has no WebGPU. Changing it reloads the page."],["Quality","Auto adapts to the size of the scene. High always draws at full sharpness. Low draws coarser, for scenes with millions of triangles."],["Auto-instance duplicates","Draw repeated shapes as one mesh with many positions. It applies the next time a model is loaded."],["Share materials by colour","Let parts of the same colour share one material. It applies the next time a model is loaded."],["Skip tiny parts","While the view moves, leave out parts that would be only a few pixels across."],["Lower the resolution if it stutters","While a heavy scene moves, draw it a little coarser, and sharp again when it stops."]]],["h","Scene"],["p","These belong to the open scene and are saved with it. [[Ctrl + ;]] opens this section directly."],["terms",[["Display units","The unit used for sizes and distances on screen: millimetres, centimetres, metres, inches, feet, or none."],["Up axis (viewport)","Which direction is up: Z, as CAD programs have it, or Y, as glTF and some 3D programs have it."],["Scene scale (×)","A multiplier for the whole scene. 0.001 turns millimetres into metres."],["Lighting","Exposure, ambient light, the sun’s strength and direction, and shadows."],["Grid","The size of a grid cell, and whether the move tool snaps to the grid."]]],["h","Storage"],["p","What the app remembers on this computer, each with a button to clear it:"],["terms",[["Recent files","The list on the start screen."],["File access","Permissions that let the app reopen a recent file without asking where it is."],["Panel sizes","The widths of the sidebars and the heights of the docks."],["All settings","Puts every preference under General and Camera back to its default."]]],["see",["move-the-view","view-modes","privacy-offline"]]]},{"slug":"read-the-numbers","group":"lighter","title":"How to read the numbers","summary":"What a triangle count tells you, where the app shows it, how to find the heaviest parts, and how to see what your work has saved.","blocks":[["p","A 3D model on a screen is made of triangles. Every triangle has to be stored in the file and drawn many times a second. So the triangle count is the simplest measure of how heavy a model is: fewer triangles mean a smaller file and a smoother view."],["p","There is no single right number. It depends on where the model will be shown. Ask whoever will use the file what they need, then work towards that."],["h","The whole scene"],["p","With nothing selected, {{Properties}} in the right sidebar describes the scene:"],["terms",[["The large number","Triangles in the scene (“tris”)."],["% of original","How many triangles are left compared with the file as it was opened. It starts at 100%."],["per part","The average number of triangles in a part."],["Parts","How many parts there are."],["Vertices","How many corner points the triangles have."],["Size","How large the whole model is along its three axes, in the display unit."],["Mesh data","How much memory the triangles take. A shape that repeats is counted once."]]],["p","The status bar under the viewport repeats the main totals."],["h","One part"],["p","Select a part and {{Properties}} describes it instead: its name, its material, its triangle count, its share of the scene, and where it ranks by weight, for example “#28 of 67 by weight”. Below that are its vertices, size and volume."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-inspect.webp","One part selected: its triangles, its share of the scene and its rank by weight."],["p","Every part remembers the triangle count it arrived with. After you have reduced a part, the card leads with what was saved, in the form “21,688 triangles −34% · was 32,728 · saved 11,040”."],["h","What an action saved"],["p","Right after an action that removes triangles, the saving shows beside the scene’s triangle count for a few seconds, for example “−427,543 (−38%)”."],["h","Find the heavy parts"],["steps",["Open the card {{Heavy parts}} in the right sidebar.","It lists the parts with the most triangles, heaviest first, each with a bar that shows how it compares with the heaviest.","Click a row. The part is selected and the view moves to it.","Decide what to do with it: delete it, simplify it, or leave it."]],["p","A few parts often hold a large share of the triangles. Start with those."],["h","Heatmap"],["p","Press [[4]] for the heatmap view. Each part is coloured by how densely it is covered in triangles for its size. Dense parts stand out in warm colours. A small part in a hot colour carries far more detail than it needs. Press [[1]] to go back to the normal view."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-heatmap.webp","Heatmap: parts coloured by how dense their triangles are."],["h","The optimisation report"],["p","The report puts the scene as it was opened beside the scene as it is now. It is the number to show whoever asked for a lighter file."],["steps",["Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} and press {{Optimisation report}}. The search ([[Ctrl + K]]) finds it too.","Read the table: {{Original}}, {{Now}} and {{Change}} for triangles, vertices, parts, draw calls, materials and mesh data in memory.","The row {{File}} fills in after you have exported.","Press {{Copy}} to copy the table as text."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-report.webp","The optimisation report: the scene as it was opened beside the scene now."],["see",["workflow","delete-small-parts","fit-to-budget","view-modes"]]]},{"slug":"delete-small-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Delete small parts","summary":"Remove the screws, washers and clips that are too small to matter, with a slider and a preview of what will go.","blocks":[["p","A CAD assembly is mostly small things: screws, washers, clips, nuts. From a normal viewing distance nobody sees them, but there are hundreds of them and each one costs triangles. This card removes every part below a size you choose."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Early, when the model is for looking at as a whole and the small hardware does not matter. Do not use it when the fasteners are the point of the picture."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Delete small parts}} in the right sidebar, under Clean up.","Drag the slider {{Smaller than}}. The value is a share of the whole model’s size. It starts at 2%.","Read the line under it. It says how many parts would go.","Press {{Isolate}}. Only the parts that would be deleted are shown, so you can check them.","Drag the slider again if needed. While the small parts are isolated, the view follows the slider.","Press {{Delete}}. The button says how many small parts it will remove.","Press {{Show all}} to see the rest of the model again."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Smaller than","The threshold, in percent of the model’s size. A part below it counts as small."],["Measured by: Diagonal","The distance from corner to corner of the part’s bounding box. The usual choice."],["Measured by: Longest side","The part’s longest side. It keeps long thin parts such as rods and pipes, and is stricter with flat ones."],["Measured by: Volume","The volume of the part’s bounding box. It catches thin sheets and wires however long they are."]]],["h","See the small parts without deleting them"],["p","The button with the warning triangle on the viewport’s tool strip tints every part under the threshold. The same switch is {{Highlight small parts}} under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}}."],["h","Undo"],["p","The delete is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and the parts are back."],["note","A common mistake is a threshold that is too high. At a few percent, handles, knobs, feet and hinges count as small too. Always press {{Isolate}} and look before you delete."],["see",["select-hidden-parts","read-the-numbers","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"select-hidden-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Select hidden parts","summary":"Find every part that cannot be seen from outside the model, such as screws inside a housing, and delete them in one step.","blocks":[["p","Much of an assembly is inside it: the screws in a housing, the board under a cover, a bearing in its seat. If the model is only ever seen from outside, those parts are dead weight. This command looks at the model from every side and selects the parts that never show."],["p","On the rear derailleur used in these pictures it found one part, 3,160 triangles, tucked inside the housing. On a bigger assembly there are usually many more."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden-result.webp","One part inside the housing can’t be seen from any side: 3,160 triangles, selected."],["h","When to use it"],["p","When the model will be shown closed, from outside. Not when someone will open a cover, look through a window in the casing, or show an exploded view."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click the {{Select hidden parts}} button on the bottom toolbar. You can also find it in the `…` list or with the search. Its panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Leave {{Whole scene}} chosen.","Press {{Select hidden parts}}, or [[Enter]].","The hidden parts are selected. {{Properties}} shows how many triangles they hold.","Press {{Isolate}} in the panel to look at them on their own. Check that nothing you need is among them.","Press {{Delete}} in the panel to remove them."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden.webp","Select hidden parts: the command panel beside the viewport."],["note","The command only selects. Nothing is deleted until you press {{Delete}}."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Whole scene / Selection only","Check every visible part, or only the parts you selected. With Selection only, the rest of the scene still counts as something that hides them."],["Accuracy","Under {{Advanced}}. How many directions the scene is looked at from: Fast, Normal or Thorough. More directions find parts that show only through a narrow gap, and take longer."],["See-through parts hide what is behind","Off: a see-through part, such as glass or a clear cover, hides nothing, so what is behind it counts as visible. On: it hides what is behind it like any other part."],["Add to the selection","Keeps what is selected now and adds the hidden parts to it."],["Largest part, of model size","Keeps only hidden parts up to this share of the model’s size. Empty means any size."],["Name contains","Keeps only hidden parts whose name contains this text, such as `screw` or `bearing`. Empty means any name."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","Selecting changes nothing. The delete is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Show all parts first ([[Alt + H]]). The check looks at visible parts.","If a part that you can see through a small opening was selected, run it again with {{Accuracy}} on Thorough.","To remove only the hidden fasteners and keep hidden structure, type part of their name in {{Name contains}}, or set a small value in {{Largest part, of model size}}."]],["see",["delete-small-parts","hide-isolate","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"fill-holes","group":"lighter","title":"Fill holes","summary":"Close bolt holes, slots, pockets and lettering in flat faces. Each closed hole removes the triangles of its walls.","blocks":[["p","A plate with forty bolt holes carries forty small cylinders of triangles that nobody notices from a distance. Fill holes closes holes in flat faces and leaves the rest of each part exactly as it was. The part keeps its outline and loses the triangles inside the holes."],["p","With the limit at 12 mm it closes bolt holes, slots and pockets up to that size, and leaves bosses and the bore of a washer or a nut alone."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-fill-holes.webp","Fill holes: closes bolt holes, slots and pockets in flat faces and leaves the rest alone."],["h","What it closes, and what it leaves"],["list",["Closed: holes that go right through, holes with a floor, counterbores, slots, pockets and engraved lettering in flat faces.","Left alone: a boss or a pin that stands up from a face.","Left alone: an opening that is what the part is, such as the bore of a washer, a nut or a bushing.","Left alone: an opening that leads into a hollow space or out through a curved surface.","Left alone: holes in curved surfaces. The tool works on flat faces only."]],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to work on. With nothing selected, it works on every visible part.","Press [[P]], or click {{Fill holes}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Set {{up to}} to the largest hole you want closed, in millimetres, measured across the hole’s widest point. It starts at 12.","Press {{Fill holes}}, or [[Enter]].","The holes close. The app reports how many, and the saving shows beside the triangle count."]],["h","What the advanced options mean"],["p","Open {{Advanced}} in the panel."],["terms",[["Through holes","Holes that go right through: an opening on both sides."],["Blind holes and pockets","Holes and pockets with a floor: an opening on one side only."],["Open holes","Openings with nothing behind them: a missing patch in a flat face."],["Raised lettering and logos","Lettering and marks that stand proud of a flat face are flattened into it. Bosses, pins and ribs are taller than the limit and stay."],["Raised, up to","A raised detail is flattened only if it stands no higher than this above its face. Embossed type is usually 0.1 to 0.5 mm high."],["Keep openings that shape the part","On: an opening that takes up much of its face and of the part is kept, such as the bore of a washer or a window in a frame. Off: only the size limit decides."],["Smallest hole","Holes narrower than this are left alone."],["Depth limit","How far the inside of a hole may reach, as a multiple of the size limit. Lower it to leave deep bores alone."],["Flatness","How far apart the directions of two triangles may be and still count as one flat face. Raise it for meshes whose flat faces are slightly bent."],["Reset to defaults","Puts every option back."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","A whole run is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and every hole is back."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Start with a small limit, look at the result, and raise it. A limit that is too high closes openings you wanted, such as a cable cut-out.","The filled result is what gets saved and exported.","A hole that was formed by pushing through the sheet, with a collar on the other side, is closed, and its collar stays as a small stub."]],["see",["smart-fit","decimate","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"split","group":"lighter","title":"Split","summary":"Take apart a mesh that arrived as one piece but is really several separate solids, so each can be selected on its own.","blocks":[["p","Some files arrive with several solid pieces fused into one part: a bracket, its pin and two bolts as a single row in the tree. You cannot hide, delete or simplify one of them without the others. Split finds the pieces that do not touch and makes each one a part of its own."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-split.webp","Split: recovers the separate solids of a mesh that was fused on export."],["h","When to use it"],["p","When clicking one thing selects several things that are clearly separate objects. An STL file, for example, holds a whole model as a single mesh."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the part that should come apart.","Press [[X]], or click {{Split}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens.","Read the preview line in the panel. It says what the split would produce.","Press {{Split selected}}, or [[Enter]].","The part is replaced by its pieces in the tree. A message says how many parts were made."]],["p","To check every part of the model at once, press {{Scan whole model}} instead."],["h","What the options mean"],["p","The four buttons at the top set how readily pieces are told apart:"],["terms",[["Strict","The most pieces. Nothing is joined. Use it if you suspect the file has doubled points along its edges."],["Tight","The default. Points that are exact duplicates are joined, which keeps one solid whole along its sharp edges without joining neighbouring solids."],["Normal","Also joins points that are a tiny distance apart, to allow for small rounding differences."],["Loose","Joins points within 0.1% of the model’s size, so solids that touch are joined on purpose. Use it when you want fewer, larger parts."]]],["p","{{Algorithm}} chooses how connection is decided:"],["terms",[["Vertex connectivity","The default. Triangles that share a corner point belong together."],["Edge connectivity","Stricter. Triangles belong together only when they share a whole edge, so solids that meet at a single point stay separate."],["Spatial proximity (AABB)","Groups triangles by where they are in space instead of how they are connected. Useful when the connections in the file are fused but the solids are still apart."],["Hybrid (vertex + spatial)","Vertex connectivity first, then a second pass by position on any piece that still holds more than half of the mesh."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","One split is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] puts the part back together."],["note","If the message says no separable parts were found, the mesh is one connected solid at this setting. Try {{Strict}}, or the {{Spatial proximity (AABB)}} algorithm."],["see",["parts-tree","select-parts","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"smart-fit","group":"lighter","title":"Smart fit","summary":"Replace a detailed part with a simple stand-in of the same size and position: a box, a cylinder, a few boxes, or blocks that keep its outline.","blocks":[["p","Some parts only need to be there. A bolt can carry a great many triangles, and from a distance a plain cylinder looks the same. Smart fit replaces the selected parts with a simple shape of the same size, in the same place."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-smart-fit.webp","Smart fit: box, turned box, cylinder, a few boxes, or blocks at the detail you choose."],["h","When to use it"],["p","For nuts, bolts, pins and housings: parts that fill a space but whose detail does not matter. Not for parts whose shape people will look at. This is the most drastic of the reducing tools: the part’s own mesh is thrown away."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to replace.","Press [[Ctrl + B]], or click {{Smart fit}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens.","Choose a shape, or leave it on {{Auto}}.","Press {{Smart fit}}, or [[Enter]].","Each selected part becomes its stand-in. Its size and position are kept."]],["h","The shapes"],["terms",[["Auto","Picks a box, a turned box or a cylinder for each part, whichever fits it best."],["Box","A box lined up with the scene’s axes."],["Turned box","A box turned to follow the part. Better for parts that sit at an angle."],["Cylinder","A cylinder along the part’s long axis."],["A few boxes","A handful of boxes, each sized and turned to one piece of the part. A levelling foot becomes a slab and a bar, a frame becomes four bars. Each box is 12 triangles."],["Blocks","The part rebuilt from a coarse grid of boxes. It looks boxy, but its steps, arms and openings stay where they are."]]],["p","One box says where a part is and nothing about what it is: a bracket becomes a brick. {{A few boxes}} and {{Blocks}} keep the outline. Both fall back to a plain box when they would not be clearly lighter than the part."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-blocks.webp","Smart fit with Blocks on the two heaviest parts of the derailleur: 172,894 triangles down to 114, the outline kept."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["At most … boxes","For {{A few boxes}}: the most boxes a part may become. Simple parts use fewer. It starts at 6."],["Blocks detail","For {{Blocks}}: how many cells the part’s longest side is cut into. More cells follow the outline more closely and cost more triangles. An opening narrower than a cell closes. It starts at 8."],["Roundness for a cylinder","Under {{Advanced}}. How round a part must be, looking down its axis, to become a cylinder. 1 is a perfect circle, a hexagon is 0.87, a square 0.71."],["Cylinder to box volume","A cylinder is chosen only if its volume is at most this share of the best box’s volume."],["Shortest cylinder, length to width","How many times longer than it is wide a part must be to become a cylinder. 0 lets flat round parts such as gears and washers be cylinders too."],["Find the part's own axes","Works out which way each part points from its mesh. Off: only the scene’s axes and the axes stored with the part are tried."]]],["h","Every part at once"],["p","The button {{Smart fit all parts}} in the card {{Reduce triangles}} replaces every part in the scene with a box, a turned box or a cylinder. It removes most of the detail of the model. Use it only when you need the roughest possible stand-in for a whole assembly."],["h","Undo"],["p","One run is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] brings the original meshes back."],["note","A quick way to simplify all fasteners of one kind: select one, press {{Select similar}} in the card {{Selection & actions}}, then run Smart fit with {{Cylinder}}."],["see",["decimate","select-parts","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"decimate","group":"lighter","title":"Decimate","summary":"Remove a share of the triangles from the selected parts while keeping their shape. Choose a percentage or a target, and preview first.","blocks":[["p","Decimate thins out the triangles of a part while keeping its overall shape. It is the tool for a part that matters and is simply too dense."],["p","It is lossy: the removed detail is gone from the mesh. It can be undone."],["h","When to use it"],["p","On the heavy parts you found in [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers), after you have deleted what is not needed. For a whole scene at once, [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget) is easier."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to reduce.","Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} in the right sidebar, under Reduce.","In the list beside {{Decimate}}, choose how much to remove: −25%, −50%, −75% or −90%.","Press {{Preview…}} under it. A table shows each part with its triangle count now and after.","Press {{Decimate}}. The button shows how many parts are selected.","Look at the parts. If they have lost too much, press [[Ctrl + Z]] and try a smaller percentage."]],["h","A triangle target instead of a percentage"],["steps",["Choose {{Target…}} in the list.","Type the number of triangles that everything selected may have together.","A line under the field says what will happen, in the form “12,400 → about 6,200 triangles”.","Press {{Decimate}}. Each selected part gives up the same share."]],["h","What it keeps"],["list",["Normals, so the shading stays smooth.","UVs, the coordinates that place a texture on a surface.","Vertex colours.","Material ranges, for a part that uses more than one material."]],["h","Good to know"],["list",["It runs in the background, several parts at a time. The window stays usable, and a long run can be stopped.","The reduced mesh is what gets saved and exported.","Decimate has no keyboard shortcut on purpose, because it is lossy. Find it in the card, in the `…` list or with the search."]],["note","A common mistake is to decimate everything by 90% at once. Curved parts become visibly faceted. Reduce in steps, and reduce the densest parts more than the rest."],["see",["fit-to-budget","read-the-numbers","smart-fit"]]]},{"slug":"fit-to-budget","group":"lighter","title":"Fit to budget","summary":"Type the number of triangles the whole scene may have. The densest meshes are reduced first until the total fits.","blocks":[["p","Often you are given one number: “the model must be under 500,000 triangles”. Fit to budget takes that number and decides for you which parts to reduce and by how much. The densest meshes give up the most. Large, sparse ones are left alone."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Near the end, after you have deleted the parts that are not needed. Deleting is free. Reducing costs detail, so reduce only what is left."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} in the right sidebar.","Type the budget in the field beside {{Fit to budget}}. You can write `150000`, `150k` or `1.5m`.","Press {{Preview…}}. A table lists the meshes that will be reduced, with their triangle count now and after, and an estimate of the new total.","Press {{Fit to budget}}.","A progress card appears. {{Cancel}} stops it, and then nothing is changed.","A message gives the result, for example how many triangles the scene went from and to, and how many meshes were reduced."]],["h","What to expect"],["list",["The preview is an estimate. A mesh can stop a little short of its share.","Every mesh keeps at least 16 triangles. If the budget is lower than the scene can reach, the app goes as far as it can and says that the budget was not reached.","If the scene is already under the budget, the app says so and changes nothing."]],["h","Undo"],["p","The whole step is one undo: [[Ctrl + Z]] restores every mesh."],["note","If the result looks too rough, undo, delete more of what is not needed (see [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts) and [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)), and run it again. The same budget then leaves more triangles for the parts that remain."],["see",["decimate","read-the-numbers","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"cleanup","group":"lighter","title":"Auto cleanup and Tidy geometry","summary":"Small one-click repairs: remove empty parts and empty groups, fix shading, recentre the model and make moves permanent.","blocks":[["p","Two cards in the right sidebar, under Clean up, hold one-click commands. Each is one undo step."],["h","Auto cleanup"],["terms",[["Remove empty parts","Deletes parts that contain no triangles. Files often carry a few of these."],["Fix degenerate parts","Removes parts whose triangles have no area, which can confuse the programs that draw them."],["Delete empty groups","Removes groups that have no parts left in them."],["Deduplicate geometry","Looks for parts whose mesh data is identical to another part’s. See the note below before you use it."]]],["p","Each command reports what it did, or says that it found nothing."],["note","Be careful with {{Deduplicate geometry}} on an assembly. It removes a part only when another part with identical mesh data sits in the same place (two copies stacked on each other), so a set of identical bolts placed in different positions stays. Check the parts count afterwards, and press [[Ctrl + Z]] if it took parts you wanted. (Before v0.20.0 it removed every repeat, placed or not; that was a bug.)"],["h","Tidy geometry"],["terms",[["Recompute smooth normals","Works out the shading directions of every surface again. Use it when parts look oddly faceted or dark after large changes."],["Recentre on origin","Moves the model so the centre of its bounding box sits at 0,0,0."],["Bake gizmo transforms","Makes the moves, turns and scalings you did with the gizmo permanent in the mesh itself."],["Centre pivot","Moves the pivot of each selected part to the centre of its bounding box. The part does not move on screen."]]],["h","Flags"],["p","The card {{Filters & flags}}, under Inspect, marks suspicious parts without changing them:"],["terms",[["Flag low-triangle parts","Marks parts with fewer triangles than the slider says."],["Flag thin slivers","Marks parts that are extremely long for their width, such as gaskets, wires and decals."]]],["p","The status bar shows how many parts are flagged. Click that number to isolate them, look at them, and decide what to do."],["see",["parts-tree","align-to-floor","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"workflow","group":"lighter","title":"A sensible order to work in","summary":"A suggested routine for a first clean-up: look, delete what is not needed, simplify what remains, check, save and export.","blocks":[["p","This is a suggestion, not a rule. It follows from what the tools do: deleting a part costs nothing in quality, so delete first. Reducing a mesh costs detail, so reduce only what you decided to keep. And there is no point in simplifying a part you are about to delete."],["steps",["Open the file and look at it. Read the totals in {{Properties}}. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers).","If the model lies on its side or sits far from the grid, stand it up with [Align to floor](#align-to-floor).","Find out where the weight is: open {{Heavy parts}} and press [[4]] for the heatmap.","Remove what nobody will see with [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts). Isolate, check, delete.","Remove small hardware with [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts), if the model does not need it.","Close holes with [Fill holes](#fill-holes).","Replace fasteners and other filler parts with stand-ins, using [Smart fit](#smart-fit).","Reduce the heavy parts that remain with [Decimate](#decimate), or bring the whole scene to a number with [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget).","Tidy up: {{Remove empty parts}} and {{Delete empty groups}} in the card {{Auto cleanup}}.","Look at the whole model again in the normal view ([[1]]) and in clay ([[5]]). Open the {{Optimisation report}} to see what you saved.","Save the scene, so you can come back to it. See [Save a scene](#save-scene).","Export the file for the program it is meant for. See [Export](#export)."]],["h","Habits that help"],["list",["Check before you delete. {{Isolate}} shows exactly what a tool has selected.","Work in steps and look after each one. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes back any of them.","Save a scene before a drastic step. Your original file is never touched, and a saved scene is a second safety net.","Stop when the number is reached. A model that is lighter than it needs to be has lost detail for nothing."]],["see",["read-the-numbers","save-scene","export"]]]},{"slug":"view-modes","group":"look","title":"View modes","summary":"Five ways to draw the same model: solid, wireframe, X-ray, heatmap and clay. One key each, and what each is good for.","blocks":[["p","A view mode changes how the model is drawn, not the model itself. Switch with the number keys, or with the buttons on the tool strip at the left edge of the viewport."],["keys",[["1","Solid: the normal view, with colours and lighting"],["2","Wireframe: only the edges of the triangles"],["3","X-ray: every part as a see-through shell"],["4","Heatmap: parts coloured by triangle density"],["5","Clay: every part in one plain material. Press it again to return to solid."]]],["h","Solid"],["p","The everyday view. Parts have their own colours and are lit by a soft studio and a light that follows the camera, so dark parts keep an edge and a sheen."],["h","Wireframe"],["p","Shows the triangles themselves. Use it to see how dense a mesh is, and to check what Decimate has done. Where the lines are so close that they merge into a solid patch, the mesh is very dense."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-wireframe.webp","Wireframe: every edge of every part."],["h","X-ray"],["p","Makes every part translucent, so you can see what is inside without hiding anything."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-xray.webp","X-ray: the whole assembly as translucent shells."],["h","Heatmap"],["p","Colours each part by how many triangles it has for its size. It answers the question “where is the weight?” at a glance. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers)."],["h","Clay"],["p","Takes the colours away. Every part gets the same plain material, so all that is left is form: edges, curvature, and what stands in front of what. It is a good view for judging whether a simplified model still reads as the same object."],["p","Right-click the clay button on the tool strip for other looks: porcelain, steel or red wax."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-clay.webp","Clay: every part in one plain material, so only the form is left."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["A view mode never reaches a file. Saving and exporting switch to solid while they run, then switch back.","To see inside a model, use X-ray, [Hide, isolate, show all](#hide-isolate) or [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts).","The background and the lighting are under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}} and {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}."]],["see",["read-the-numbers","hide-isolate","screenshots"]]]},{"slug":"colours-materials","group":"look","title":"Colours and materials","summary":"Open the materials dock to see every material in the scene, recolour all the parts that share one, and assign materials to parts.","blocks":[["p","A material is what a surface looks like: its colour, and how rough or metallic it is. Parts share materials. A CAD file with fifty grey brackets usually has one grey material that all fifty use. Change that material and all fifty change."],["h","Open the materials dock"],["p","Press [[Shift + M]], or click the palette button on the bottom toolbar. The dock slides up from the bottom of the window and shows every material as a small ball, with the number of parts that use it."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-materials.webp","The materials dock: every material in the scene, with how many parts use it."],["h","Find a material"],["list",["Type in {{Filter…}} to filter by name, by colour (as a hex code such as `#ff6600`) or by type.","Choose the order: {{Most used}}, {{Most triangles}}, {{Name}} or {{Colour}}.","Switch between a grid and a list with the two small buttons.","Select a part in the viewport and look at {{Properties}}: it shows the part’s material."]],["h","See where a material is used"],["steps",["Click a material in the dock.","The inspector at the right of the dock shows its colour, roughness, metalness and opacity, and how many parts and triangles use it.","Press {{Select parts}} to select every part that uses it, or {{Isolate}} to see only those parts."]],["h","Recolour"],["steps",["Double-click a material in the dock, or select it and press {{Edit}} in the inspector.","Change the colour in the editor that opens. You can also change how rough or metallic the surface is.","Every part that shares the material changes at once."]],["terms",[["Roughness","How matt the surface is. Low is glossy, high is dull."],["Metalness","How much the surface behaves like metal."],["Opacity","How solid the surface is. Lower values are see-through."]]],["h","Give parts a different material"],["steps",["Select the parts in the viewport or in the tree.","Click the material you want in the dock.","Press {{Assign}} in the inspector."]],["h","Make, copy, merge, delete"],["p","The buttons at the right of the dock’s header:"],["terms",[["New material","Makes a new material. If parts are selected, they get it."],["Duplicate","Copies the picked material."],["Merge","Turns several picked materials into one. Hold [[Ctrl]] and click to pick several."],["Delete","Deletes the picked materials."],["Industry presets","Applies a ready-made look to the picked material, such as brushed steel, glass or rubber."]]],["p","A right-click on a material gives the same commands, and one to copy its colour code."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["A new material is named after its colour, and the name follows the colour when you change it.","Every change in the dock is an undo step.","Drag the top edge of the dock to make it taller. Double-click the edge to reset it.","The dock and the console share the same place and take turns."]],["see",["view-modes","select-parts","export"]]]},{"slug":"move-rotate-scale","group":"look","title":"Move, rotate, scale and measure","summary":"Use the gizmo to move, turn and resize parts, type exact values in the transform panel, and measure the distance between two points.","blocks":[["p","Select a part and a gizmo appears on it: a set of coloured handles. Red is the X axis, green is Y and blue is Z. Drag a handle to change the part along that axis."],["keys",[["E","Move"],["R","Rotate"],["T","Scale"],["Q","Hide the gizmo"],["Shift","Hold while dragging to snap to round steps"]]],["h","Move a part"],["steps",["Select the part. To move several parts together, select them all, or group them first with [[Ctrl + G]].","Press [[E]].","Drag one of the arrows. A small read-out shows how far the part has moved.","Hold [[Shift]] while you drag to move in round steps sized to the model."]],["p","Rotate ([[R]]) and scale ([[T]]) work the same way. With [[Shift]] held, rotation snaps to 15° and scale to steps of 0.1."],["h","Type exact values"],["steps",["Click the transform panel button on the viewport’s tool strip, the fourth button from the top. The {{Transform}} panel appears at the foot of the left sidebar.","It has three columns, {{Position}}, {{Rotation}} and {{Size}}, with a row for X, Y and Z.","Click a number and type a value, or drag it sideways.","Press {{Reset}} to put position, rotation and size back."]],["p","Right-click a column heading to copy all three values or paste them onto another part. The units follow {{Display units}} under {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}."],["h","Copy and duplicate"],["keys",[["Ctrl + D","Duplicate the selection"],["Ctrl + C","Copy the selection"],["Ctrl + V","Paste"]]],["h","Lock a part"],["p","Right-click a part in the tree and choose {{Lock}}. A locked part cannot be moved, and delete skips it. Choose {{Unlock}} in the same menu to release it."],["h","Measure"],["steps",["Press [[M]], or click the ruler on the bottom toolbar.","Click a point on the model. A dot follows the pointer to show where you will pick.","Click a second point. The distance appears between them.","Measure again, or press [[M]] or [[Esc]] to leave measure mode."]],["list",["Dragging still turns the view while you measure.","Measurements stay on screen after you leave measure mode, and are listed in the card {{Measurements}} in the right sidebar.","{{Clear all}} in that card removes them."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Moves, turns and scalings are undo steps, whether you dragged the gizmo or typed a value."],["note","Moving a part with the gizmo changes where it sits, not its mesh. To write the change into the mesh itself, use {{Bake gizmo transforms}} in the card {{Tidy geometry}}. Most people never need to."],["see",["align-to-floor","select-parts","cleanup"]]]},{"slug":"align-to-floor","group":"look","title":"Align to floor","summary":"Stand a model upright on the grid, centred over the origin, when it arrived on its side or far away from the centre of the scene.","blocks":[["p","Files arrive anywhere and any way up: a part modelled 40 metres from its origin, an assembly lying on its side. Align to floor turns the whole model in steps and puts it on the grid, over the point 0,0,0."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-align.webp","Align to floor: turn the model in steps and choose which side of it sits at zero."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Right after opening a file, before anything else, if the model is not standing the way you expect. A model that sits on the floor at the origin is easier to orbit, and easier to place in the next program."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Align to floor}} in the right sidebar, under Clean up.","If the model lies on its side, use the turn buttons. Each of the rows X, Y and Z has two arrows that turn the whole model about that axis, one step back or one step forward. A step is 90° to begin with.","Press them until the model stands the right way up. You watch it move.","Press {{Align to floor}}. The model moves so that it stands on the grid with its middle over 0,0,0."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Turn, step","How far one press turns the model. 90 gives quarter turns. A small number straightens a model that is slightly tilted."],["Sits at zero with its: X and Y","Which side of the model goes to 0 on that axis: {{Low side}}, {{Middle}}, {{High side}}, or {{Keep}} to leave it where it is."],["Sits at zero with its: Up","{{Floor}} stands the model on the grid. {{Top}} hangs it from the grid. {{Middle}} centres it. {{Keep}} leaves its height alone."],["Reset","Puts the model back where and how it was loaded."],["Place every file I open","Places every file as it is opened, with the choices above. It only moves the model. It does not turn it."]]],["p","Middle, Middle and Floor is the usual choice: it stands the model on the grid, centred over the origin."],["h","Undo"],["p","A whole run of adjustments is one undo step. {{Reset}} in the card goes back to how the file was loaded."],["note","Which direction is “up” is a setting of the scene: {{Up axis (viewport)}} under {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}. CAD files normally use Z as up. If the whole scene looks turned by a quarter, check that setting before you turn the model."],["see",["move-rotate-scale","export","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"exploded-view","group":"look","title":"Exploded view","summary":"Push the parts of an assembly apart with a slider, to see how it is put together.","blocks":[["p","An exploded view moves every part away from the centre of the model, so parts that sit inside or behind others come into the open. It is a way of looking, like in an assembly manual."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-exploded.webp","Exploded view, with a slider for each axis."],["steps",["Open the card {{Exploded view}} in the right sidebar, under Inspect.","Drag {{All axes}} to push the parts apart in every direction.","Or drag {{X axis}}, {{Y axis}} or {{Z axis}} to spread them along one direction only.","Press {{Reset all}} to bring every part back."]],["p","The values are percentages. You can click a number and type one."],["note","A suggestion: put the sliders back to 0 before you edit, save or export. The exploded view is for looking, and a model is easier to work on when its parts are where they belong."],["see",["hide-isolate","view-modes","screenshots"]]]},{"slug":"library","group":"look","title":"The parts library and shapes","summary":"Add standard parts to a scene: 86 bolts, nuts, pipes, profiles and machine parts, each with sizes you can change afterwards.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser can also add parts. The library holds 86 ready-made parts. Each one is parametric, which means it is built from numbers such as a diameter and a length, and you can change those numbers after you have placed it."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-library.webp","The library drawer: a pillow block and a handwheel placed, and a stepper motor on its way in, shown where it will stand."],["h","Open the library"],["p","The library is a drawer under the viewport. Click {{Library}} on the bottom toolbar, or press [[Shift + L]]. Click a part to look at it and set its size, then add it. The drawer closes with the same button, and it shares the space with the materials drawer: opening one puts the other away."],["h","What is in it"],["terms",[["Shapes","Cube, sphere, cylinder, cone, torus, capsule, wedge, rounded box, star and more."],["Fasteners","Hex bolt, Allen screw, countersunk and button head screws, carriage bolt, U-bolt, eye bolt, set screw, threaded rod, rivet, washers, retaining ring, standoff."],["Nuts","Hex, lock, jam, flange, cap, castle, wing, thumb, square, coupling, eye and T-slot nuts."],["Pipes and flanges","Tube, elbow, tee, cross, reducer, pipe cap, flange."],["Profiles and plates","I-beam, box tube, angle, C-channel, T and Z profiles, T-slot extrusion, flat bar, round and hex bar, perforated and slotted plate, gusset, hinge."],["Machine parts","Gear, rack, sprocket, pulley, bearing, bushing, pillow block, shaft collar, linear rail, spring, handwheel, star knob, pull handle, levelling foot, wheel, enclosure, stepper motor."]]],["p","The parts you used last wait on a shelf at the top. {{Search the library…}} filters the shelves as you type."],["h","Add a part"],["steps",["For a fastener, first choose its thread size and length at the top of the library. M6 and 24 mm to begin with. Every screw, nut and washer you add comes out in that size.","Click a part to add it. It lands on the selected part, or on the floor at the origin when nothing is selected.","Or drag a part into the viewport. A see-through copy follows the pointer and shows where the part will stand: square to the face under the pointer, or upright on the floor.","Let go. The part is there, with a row of its own in the parts tree."]],["h","Change it afterwards"],["steps",["Select the part.","Look at {{Properties}} in the right sidebar. It now has a section called {{Shape parameters}}.","Drag a number sideways to change it, or click it and type. Hold [[Shift]] to go ten times faster and [[Alt]] to go ten times finer.","The part rebuilds as you go."]],["h","A bolt with its own nut"],["p","A hex bolt, an Allen screw, a threaded rod or an eye bolt can carry a nut."],["steps",["Select the bolt.","In {{Shape parameters}}, switch on {{Nut on the bolt}}.","Choose the kind in {{Nut}}: a hex nut, a wing nut, a castle nut and so on.","Set {{Nut from the head}}: how far from the head the nut sits."]],["p","The nut is part of the bolt’s own shape. It follows the bolt’s size and length, stays one row in the tree, and cannot sit past the end of the thread."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-bolt-nut.webp","A hex bolt that carries its own nut: size, length and the nut’s distance from the head are all parameters."],["h","Other ways to add a shape"],["list",["The search: press [[Ctrl + K]], type `add flange` and press [[Enter]].","The add button on the bottom toolbar, next to the search: a click adds the shape you used last, and holding the button down opens a choice of shapes.","The start screen: pick Cube, Sphere, Cylinder or Torus to start a new scene with that shape."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Adding a part is an undo step, and so is each change to its parameters."],["note","Library parts count like any other part: they have triangles, appear in the totals, and are saved and exported with the scene."],["see",["search-and-commands","move-rotate-scale","parts-tree"]]]},{"slug":"draw-lines","group":"look","title":"Draw lines and shapes","summary":"Draw curves, rectangles, circles and polygons on the floor or on a face, edit their points, and use them as paths.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser can draw lines. A line is a curve you place yourself, point by point, or a ready-made shape such as a rectangle or a circle. It is a part of the scene like any other: it has a row in the parts tree, can be hidden, renamed, grouped and moved with the gizmo, and every change can be undone. Lines have no triangles; they are there to look at, to measure against, and to sweep a profile along (see [Sweep](#sweep))."],["h","Start drawing"],["steps",["Press [[L]], or click {{Draw}} on the bottom toolbar. The card opens bottom left in the viewport.","Choose a tool at the top of the card, and the kind of curve: {{Bezier}}, {{Cubic}}, {{B-spline}} or {{Linear}}.","Click in the viewport. The keys that do something right now are listed at the bottom right.","Press [[Esc]] to put the tool away."]],["h","The tools"],["terms",[["Pen","Click for a point; drag to pull out handles and make a curve (Bezier). [[Enter]] finishes the line, [[C]] closes it, and a click on the first point closes it too. [[Backspace]] takes back the last point."],["Rectangle","Click one corner, move, click again to place it. [[Shift]] makes a square and [[Alt]] draws from the centre."],["Circle","Click the centre, move, click again. The ellipse switch gives it two radii."],["Arc","Three clicks: the start, the end, and a point the arc passes through."],["Polygon","Click the centre, move, click again. The arrow keys change the number of sides; the card has Star and Around the circle."],["Freehand","Draw with the mouse held down; the stroke is smoothed into a curve when you let go."],["Edit","Change a line that is already there (see below)."]]],["p","While a rectangle, circle or polygon waits for its second click you can type its size, for example `20 x 10`, and press [[Enter]]."],["h","Where it draws"],["terms",[["Ground, Front, Side","The three flat planes of the scene."],["View","A plane that faces the camera."],["Surface","The face of a part: the first click picks the face, and the line is drawn on it."],["Offset","Moves the plane along its normal, in millimetres."]]],["h","Snapping"],["p","With {{Snap to grid}} on, a field of dots fades in around the pointer, and a click a few pixels from a dot lands exactly on it. {{Grid step}} is Auto (it follows the zoom) or a number you type. {{Snap to points}} snaps to the points of other lines. Holding [[Shift]] snaps the angle in steps of 15°. The dots hide themselves when they would crowd, for example when you look at the plane from far away or edge-on."],["h","Edit a line"],["steps",["Choose {{Edit}} in the card and click a line (or pick it in the tree).","Drag a point or a handle. The curve follows while you drag.","Click on the curve to add a point. Double-click a point to make it smooth or sharp. [[Delete]] removes the selected point and [[C]] opens or closes the line.","Hold [[Ctrl]] while you drag a handle to move it on its own; the other handle of that point stays where it was, and stays free afterwards.","The buttons in the card close or open the line, reverse it, simplify it, make a point smooth or sharp, round a corner (or all of them), or add a mirrored copy."]],["p","Bezier handles snap to the grid like points do."],["h","Round or cut the corners"],["p","A rectangle or polygon that is still a shape shows a small ring on its first corner in Edit. Drag it along the diagonal to round or cut every corner at once; it snaps to the grid and the card says how far the corner reaches. Click {{Round}} or {{Bevel}} next to the ring to change the kind: a true arc, or a straight cut. The same two settings are in the card and in Properties."],["h","Properties of a line"],["p","Select a line and look at {{Properties}} in the right sidebar."],["terms",[["Shape","For a rectangle, circle, ellipse, polygon or star that has not had its points moved: width, height, radius, sides, star and inner radius, corner type and size. A rectangle grows about its centre."],["Spline","Type (Bezier, Cubic, B-spline, Linear), Close Spline, and {{Interpolation}}, which says how the curve is cut into the short straight pieces that are drawn: None keeps only the points, Natural and Uniform put a number of points on each piece, Adaptive adds points until no corner turns by more than the Angle, and Subdivided does that and also keeps every piece shorter than the Maximum length. The settings that do not apply are greyed."]]],["p","Once you move a point of a shape by hand it becomes an ordinary spline, and the Shape card goes away."],["note","Lines are written into GLB, glTF and saved scenes. The other export formats have no lines and leave them out."],["see",["sweep","organise-the-tree","move-rotate-scale"]]]},{"slug":"sweep","group":"look","title":"Sweep a profile along a line","summary":"Turn a path and a profile into a solid part: a pipe, a rail, a ring.","blocks":[["p","Sweep carries a shape (the profile) along a line (the path) and makes a solid part of it. A circle along a curve makes a pipe, a rectangle along a straight line makes a bar, and a closed path makes a ring."],["steps",["Draw the path with the Draw tool (see [Draw lines and shapes](#draw-lines)). If the profile should be a drawn shape, draw that too.","Open the {{Sweep}} card from the bottom toolbar or the search ([[Ctrl + K]]).","Pick the {{Path}} in the list.","Choose the {{Profile}}: a {{Circle}} (give its size), a {{Rectangle}} (width and height) or another {{Line}} from the list.","Press {{Sweep}}."]],["p","The result is a normal part called Sweep, with a row in the tree, and one undo step."],["h","Advanced"],["terms",[["Scale","How big the profile is at the start and at the end, as a percentage. 100 and 50 make a pipe that narrows."],["Twist","How far the profile turns along the path, in degrees."],["Close the ends","Puts a lid on each end so the part is solid."],["Hard-edge angle","Corners sharper than this are kept sharp; gentler ones are shaded smooth."]]],["note","The sweep follows the line as it is drawn, as short straight pieces. A line with a coarse Interpolation (see Properties of a line) gives a coarse pipe."],["see",["draw-lines","organise-the-tree","cleanup"]]]},{"slug":"screenshots","group":"look","title":"Screenshots","summary":"Save a picture of the viewport as a PNG, at the size of the view or at any resolution up to poster size.","blocks":[["steps",["Set up the view: orbit to the angle you want and choose a view mode.","Click the camera button at the right end of the top bar. The screen flashes like a shutter and the {{Save screenshot}} window opens.","Choose a resolution.","Check the file name, or type your own.","Press {{Save…}}. Where the browser allows it, you choose the folder. Otherwise the picture goes to your downloads folder."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Resolution","Six presets: the viewport at 1×, 2× (sharp) or 4× (poster), and 1080p, 1440p and 4K."],["Custom (px)","Any width and height, from 16 to 16,384 pixels a side. The aspect ratio and the number of megapixels are shown beside it."],["View mode","{{Current}} uses the view mode that is on. {{Solid}}, {{Wireframe}} and {{X-ray}} use that mode for the picture only."],["Hide scene grid","Leaves the floor lines and the origin axes out of this picture."],["Filename","Starts as the model’s name and the time."],["Stamp info on image","Prints a small panel in a corner of the picture. Tick what it should show: model name, statistics, selection counts, timestamp, camera, units, and the app’s name. Then pick the corner."]]],["p","The picture is a PNG file. Right-clicking an empty spot of the viewport also offers {{Save screenshot…}}."],["note","A 4× picture of a large viewport is a very large file. The limit is 16,384 pixels a side."],["see",["view-modes","move-the-view","exploded-view"]]]},{"slug":"save-scene","group":"save","title":"Save a scene","summary":"Save your work as a scene file so you can carry on later. What a scene file is, and how it differs from an export.","blocks":[["p","Saving a scene keeps your work so you can open it again and carry on. Exporting writes a file for another program. Do both: save a scene for yourself, export for whoever gets the result."],["h","What a scene file is"],["p","A scene file is a `.glb` file. It holds the model as it is now, after everything you deleted, reduced and recoloured. It also remembers things only MeshOptimiser uses: the camera, the view settings, which parts were hidden or flagged, and your measurements. Other programs can open the file as an ordinary GLB and ignore the rest."],["h","Save"],["steps",["Press [[Ctrl + S]]. You can also click the icon on the scene’s tab, or choose {{Menu}}, then {{Save scene…}}.","The {{Save scene}} window opens. Check the {{Filename}}, or type your own. The ending `.glb` is added for you.","Press {{Save…}}.","Where the browser allows it, a file window opens. Choose a folder and confirm. Otherwise the file goes to your downloads folder.","The dot on the tab’s icon turns green."]],["p","Saving again under the same name writes to the same file without asking where."],["h","Know whether you have saved"],["p","The dot on the tab’s icon is yellow while there are changes that are not saved, and green when the scene is saved. Other tabs show a yellow dot too when they have unsaved changes. Closing a tab or quitting with unsaved changes asks first."],["h","Open a scene again"],["p","Open the `.glb` like any other file: drop it on the window, press [[Ctrl + O]], or pick it from the recent files on the start screen. The camera and the view settings come back with it, and hidden parts are still hidden."],["h","Scene or export?"],["terms",[["Save scene","Everything, including hidden parts, at the scene’s own scale and orientation. For continuing your work."],["Export","What you choose, in the format, scale and orientation the next program needs. For handing over."]]],["note","Your original file is never overwritten by a save, unless you choose its name and folder yourself. Give the scene a name of its own."],["see",["export","scene-tabs","undo-redo"]]]},{"slug":"export","group":"save","title":"Export","summary":"Write the model to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY or a parts list, and set the scale, up axis and origin the next program needs.","blocks":[["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click {{Export}} at the right of the top bar, or press [[Ctrl + E]]. A list of formats drops down.","Choose a format. The Export window opens with that format selected. You can still change it in the list on the left.","Set the options on the right. For a first try, the defaults are fine.","Press the button at the bottom, for example {{Export GLB}}.","The file arrives as a download in your browser, normally in your downloads folder. A message confirms it."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-export.webp","Export to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY, or a parts list."],["h","The formats"],["terms",[["GLB","Modern binary glTF, in one file. The app calls it best for the web and Cinema 4D."],["glTF","The same as GLB, written as text (JSON glTF 2.0)."],["FBX","Binary FBX 7.4. The app lists Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Houdini and Unreal."],["USDZ","For Apple Quick Look and Reality Composer."],["OBJ","Wavefront OBJ. Universal, geometry only."],["STL","Binary STL. The standard for 3D printing."],["PLY","Geometry with vertex colours."],["CSV","A parts list as a spreadsheet: name, colour, triangles, vertices and dimensions. No geometry is written."]]],["p","[Which format should I choose](#choose-format) helps you pick. The {{Compare}} button in the Export window opens a sheet that shows what each format keeps."],["h","The options"],["terms",[["Unit scale","Multiplies every size in the file. {{1.0 (no change)}} keeps the model as it is. CAD models are usually in millimetres. If the next program works in metres, choose {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}. The list also has cm to m, m to mm, m to cm, inches to mm, mm to inches, and {{Custom…}}."],["Custom scale","The multiplier, when {{Unit scale}} is on {{Custom…}}."],["Up axis","{{Z-up (CAD, keep)}} keeps Z as the up direction. {{Y-up (glTF, OBJ, Maya)}} turns the model so Y is up, which is what those formats and programs expect."],["Origin","{{Keep model origin}} leaves the model where it is. {{Recentre to bbox centre}} puts the middle of the model at 0,0,0. {{Centre XY, drop to floor (Z=0)}} centres it and stands it on the floor."],["Merge all parts into one mesh","Writes the whole model as one object. Useful for 3D printing. The parts list is lost in the file."],["Visible parts only","Leaves hidden parts out of the file. On by default."],["Selected parts only","Writes only the parts that are selected. It overrides the switch above."],["Draco compression","GLB only. Compresses the mesh data, which typically makes the file 5 to 20 times smaller. Exporting and loading are slower."],["ASCII FBX","FBX only. Writes the FBX as text instead of binary. Try it if the next program cannot read the binary file."]]],["p","For CSV, the options about scale, axis, origin and merging are dimmed, because no geometry is written."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The export contains the model as it is now: deleted parts are gone and reduced meshes are reduced.","It runs in solid view and switches back afterwards, so a view mode never ends up in the file.","After an export, the {{Optimisation report}} shows the size of the exported file beside the size of the file you opened.","Draco compression needs an internet connection the first time, because its encoder is one of the converters the app fetches on demand.","A very large model can be too big for a text format such as OBJ. The app then suggests GLB or STL, which are binary and much smaller."]],["note","A common mistake is the wrong scale. If the model arrives a thousand times too big or too small in the next program, export again with a different {{Unit scale}}."],["note","Another is a model that arrives lying on its back. Export again with the other {{Up axis}}."],["see",["choose-format","save-scene","file-formats"]]]},{"slug":"choose-format","group":"save","title":"Which format should I choose","summary":"A short guide by purpose: the web, Cinema 4D, Blender, game engines, AR on an iPhone, 3D printing, or a parts list.","blocks":[["p","If you are not sure, export GLB. It is one file, it is compact, and it keeps the parts tree, the materials and the colours. The app’s own format sheet calls it the best all-round choice."],["terms",[["A web page or a web viewer","GLB. Switch on {{Draco compression}} only if the viewer you use can read it."],["Cinema 4D","GLB. The app recommends it for Cinema 4D 2026."],["Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Houdini","FBX. These are the programs the app lists for it."],["Unreal","FBX. The app lists Unreal under FBX."],["Unity","The app does not name Unity for any format. FBX is the usual choice for game engines."],["AR on an iPhone or iPad","USDZ, the format of Apple Quick Look."],["3D printing","STL. Switch on {{Merge all parts into one mesh}} if the printer software wants one object."],["A program that reads nothing else","OBJ. It is understood almost everywhere, but it carries no hierarchy: the tree is flattened into the names of the parts."],["Research or scanning tools","PLY, which holds geometry and a colour for every vertex."],["A list of parts for a spreadsheet","CSV."],["Carrying on later in MeshOptimiser","Not an export at all: [Save a scene](#save-scene)."]]],["h","What each format keeps"],["terms",[["GLB and glTF","The parts tree, full materials, vertex colours."],["FBX","The parts tree and materials. No vertex colours."],["USDZ","The parts tree and materials. No vertex colours."],["OBJ","Materials through a separate `.mtl` file. A flat list of parts, no tree."],["STL","Geometry only, as a single mesh. No colours."],["PLY","Geometry as a single mesh, with vertex colours."],["CSV","No geometry."]]],["h","Two settings that go with the format"],["list",["Scale. A CAD model is usually in millimetres. Many 3D programs and the glTF format work in metres. If the model arrives huge, export with {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}.","Up axis. CAD uses Z as up. glTF, OBJ and Maya use Y. If the model arrives lying down, export with {{Y-up (glTF, OBJ, Maya)}}."]],["note","These are safe starting points. The program you are exporting to has the last word, so do one test export before you spend time on the model."],["see",["export","file-formats","save-scene"]]]},{"slug":"convert-step-to-glb","group":"jobs","title":"How to convert a STEP file to GLB","summary":"Convert a STEP CAD file to a small GLB for the web, on your own computer and for free. The model is never uploaded. Drop it in, check it, export.","blocks":[["p","A STEP file is how engineers share a design. A web page cannot show one. GLB is the format it can show: a compact 3D file that browsers, AR viewers and most 3D programs open. This page takes you from one to the other with MeshOptimiser, on your own computer. Nothing is uploaded, and there is nothing to pay."],["h","What you need"],["list",["MeshOptimiser, started once. See [What you need](#requirements) and [Start it the first time](#first-start).","Your model as a STEP file (`.step` or `.stp`). If it is still in your CAD program, see [Native CAD files: export a STEP file first](#cad-files-step-first)."]],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Start the app with `start.bat` (Windows) or `start.command` (macOS).","Drag the STEP file onto the window.","In the {{Import settings}} window, leave everything as it is and press {{Import}}. {{Tessellation quality}} at Standard (0.5) is the recommended choice. See [Open your first file](#first-file).","Wait while the progress card works. A big assembly can take several minutes. Repeated parts are found and stored once, and the result is compressed.","Look at the model. {{Properties}} on the right shows how heavy it is. Press [[1]] for the normal view.","Press [[Ctrl + E]] and choose {{GLB}}. In the Export window, check {{Unit scale}} and {{Up axis}} (below).","Press {{Export GLB}}. The file arrives in your downloads folder."]],["h","Two settings that save a second try"],["terms",[["Unit scale","CAD models are usually in millimetres, and glTF and most web viewers expect metres. If the model shows up a thousand times too big in the viewer, export again with {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}."],["Up axis","CAD uses Z as up. glTF uses Y. If the model lies on its back in the viewer, export with {{Y-up (glTF, OBJ, Maya)}}."]]],["p","Leave {{Draco compression}} off unless the viewer you use can read it. See [Which format should I choose](#choose-format)."],["h","How small will it be?"],["p","It depends on the model. The app’s README gives one example: a 320 MB STEP file became an 11 MB compressed GLB. Assemblies with many identical parts, such as bolts, shrink the most, because each shape is stored once. That is an example, not a promise for every file."],["p","If the GLB is still too heavy, remove what nobody will see before you export. [A sensible order to work in](#workflow) shows how, and [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget) takes a triangle count for the whole scene."],["h","Without opening the viewer"],["p","To convert a file and do nothing else, drag it onto `step2glb.bat` (Windows) or `step2glb.command` (macOS). The GLB is written next to it. Or use the command line:"],["code","python step2glb.py input.step --meshopt"],["p","The command line is described in [The command-line converter](#command-line). Meshopt compression needs the free tool gltfpack on your PATH. If a viewer cannot open a Meshopt-compressed GLB, convert again with `--no-meshopt`, or export from the app instead."],["note","A common mistake is to send the file from the `inbox` folder. That folder is where the app keeps its working copy. The file you export is the one to send, because it has your clean-up in it."],["h","Next"],["p","To see what the conversion did, read [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers). For the file formats in detail, see [Export](#export)."],["see",["first-file","export","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"remove-bolts-from-step-assembly","group":"jobs","title":"How to remove bolts and screws from a STEP assembly","summary":"Take the bolts, screws, nuts and washers out of a CAD assembly before it goes on the web. Select one, select the rest, check, delete, and undo if needed.","blocks":[["p","Fasteners are a large part of a CAD assembly. A model can hold hundreds of bolts, nuts and washers, and each carries thousands of triangles that nobody sees from a normal viewing distance. Taking them out makes the model much lighter. It also leaves your hardware out of the file you share."],["p","On the rear derailleur used in these pictures, twelve bolts and screws held 129,460 triangles, about 12% of the whole model."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-fasteners.webp","Bolts and screws found in the rear derailleur: twelve parts, 129,460 triangles. They were found with the Fasteners tool."],["p","The quickest way is the {{Fasteners}} card on the bottom toolbar: it finds bolts, screws, nuts and washers by their shape, whatever they are called, and selects them. The steps below do the same by hand, one kind at a time."],["h","The steps by hand"],["steps",["Open the STEP file. See [Open your first file](#first-file).","Click one bolt in the viewport or in the parts tree.","In the card {{Selection & actions}} in the right sidebar, press {{Select similar}}. Every part with the same shape as the bolt is selected, so all copies of that bolt are selected at once.","Press [[S]] to isolate the selection. Look at it. Only the bolts should be left on screen.","Press [[S]] again to come back, then press [[Del]] to delete them.","Do the same for the next kind of bolt, then for the nuts and the washers. Bolts of different lengths are different shapes, so each size is one round.","Press [[Alt + H]] to show everything, and look at the model once more."]],["p","Every delete is one undo step. [[Ctrl + Z]] brings the bolts back. The {{Optimisation report}} shows how many triangles you saved."],["h","Other ways to find the hardware"],["terms",[["By name","In {{Selection & actions}}, press {{By name}} and type `bolt|screw|nut|washer`. It selects every part with one of those words in its name, in capitals or not. This only works when the CAD names are honest, and they often are not: `Part_0031` tells you nothing. A short word such as nut can also match other names, so isolate before you delete."],["Delete small parts","Removes every part below a size you set, with a preview. Hardware is small, but so are other things. Press {{Isolate}} and look before you delete. See [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["Select hidden parts","Finds the screws inside a housing, because nobody can see them. To keep hidden structure and remove only hardware, type part of the name in {{Name contains}}. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."]]],["p","The next version of the app adds a Fasteners tool that finds bolts, screws, nuts and washers by their shape, whatever they are called. It is in the project’s repository and not in the released version, so this page does not rely on it."],["h","When you want the bolts to stay"],["p","If the bolts must be visible but light, do not delete them. Select them the same way, then use [Smart fit](#smart-fit) with {{Cylinder}}. A bolt becomes a plain cylinder of the same size in the same place."],["h","After they are gone"],["list",["Bolt holes stay in the plates they held. [Fill holes](#fill-holes) closes holes in flat faces, so the surfaces look whole again.","Take out the bolts that hold a cover only if you do not mind a cover that seems to float. From outside it usually looks fine."]],["note","Hide ([[H]]) is not delete. A hidden part is still in the scene, and whether it is written to a file depends on the export options. To be sure a part is not in the file, delete it."],["h","Next"],["p","To remove hardware and everything else that is hidden before you send a model to someone, read [How to make a CAD model safe to send to a customer](#make-model-safe-to-send)."],["see",["select-parts","delete-small-parts","select-hidden-parts","smart-fit"]]]},{"slug":"make-model-safe-to-send","group":"jobs","title":"How to make a CAD model safe to send to a customer","summary":"Show your product without handing over your design. Remove hidden parts and fasteners, simplify what is left, clean the names and export a small GLB.","blocks":[["p","A customer, a dealer or a web visitor needs to see your product. They do not need the screws inside it, the board under the cover or the exact shape of every internal part. This page takes a STEP assembly and turns it into a small GLB that shows the outside and little else."],["note","No file is a guarantee. Anyone can measure what they can see. This page makes the file show less, which is a good habit. It does not stop a determined person from copying what is left, and it is no replacement for an agreement with the customer."],["h","What a GLB already leaves out"],["p","A GLB holds triangles, colours and the names of parts. It has no feature tree, no sketches, no dimensions and no history of how the model was built. A STEP file has the exact surfaces. A GLB has an approximation made of small flat triangles."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Export a STEP file from your CAD program and open it. See [Native CAD files: export a STEP file first](#cad-files-step-first).","Decide what the customer should see. Usually that is the closed product, seen from outside.","Remove what is inside. Use {{Select hidden parts}}, press {{Isolate}} to check what it found, and press {{Delete}}. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts).","Remove the hardware: bolts, screws, nuts and washers. See [How to remove bolts and screws from a STEP assembly](#remove-bolts-from-step-assembly).","Simplify the parts whose shape is the secret. [Smart fit](#smart-fit) can swap a part for a box, a cylinder, a few boxes or blocks that keep its outline. {{Fill holes}} closes bolt holes and pockets.","Bring the weight down to a number with [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget), if the file must be small.","Clean the names (below).","Export a GLB. See [Export](#export) and the settings below.","Check the exported file (below)."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden-result.webp","Select hidden parts: one part inside the housing can’t be seen from any side. It is selected and ready to delete."],["h","Clean the names"],["p","Part names travel into the GLB. Names like `Gearbox_internal_v7_ACME` say more than a picture does. Select the parts and press [[F2]] to rename many at once, or use {{Flatten}} to remove levels of grouping that show how the product is built. See [The parts tree](#parts-tree)."],["p","Material names travel too. Look at them in the materials dock ([[Shift + M]]), and see [Colours and materials](#colours-materials)."],["p","Leave the extra information off when you convert a STEP file. In the import settings, keep {{Layer attributes}} and {{Validation properties (mass, area)}} switched off, so layers and per-part volume and area are not read in the first place."],["h","Export settings"],["list",["Format: GLB.","Keep {{Visible parts only}} on, which is the default.","Better still, delete what you do not want to share instead of only hiding it. A deleted part is gone from the file for certain.","Choose {{Unit scale}} and {{Up axis}} for the viewer your customer will use. See [How to convert a STEP file to GLB](#convert-step-to-glb)."]],["h","Check the file before you send it"],["steps",["Drop the exported GLB onto the app window. A file never replaces a scene that has something in it, so it opens in a new tab.","Open the parts tree and read every name.","Press [[3]] for X-ray and look inside. Nothing should be there that you took out.","Look at {{Properties}} and the {{Optimisation report}}. The parts and triangle counts should match what you expect."]],["h","What to send, and what not to send"],["list",["Send the file you exported, and only that.","Do not send the STEP file. It has the exact surfaces.","Do not send a saved scene. It keeps your view, your selection and your recolouring, and it is meant for you.","Do not send files from the `inbox` folder. Next to each converted GLB the app keeps a small `.params.json` record that includes the path of the original file on your disk."]],["h","What this does not do"],["list",["It removes whole parts, not the inside of a part. A housing keeps its inner walls, because the tool selects parts, not faces.","It does not hide what is visible. The outside of your product is in the file, and anyone can measure it.","It does not encrypt or lock the file."]],["see",["select-hidden-parts","smart-fit","export","privacy-offline"]]]},{"slug":"shortcuts","group":"reference","title":"Keyboard shortcuts","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut and mouse action in MeshOptimiser v0.20.0, grouped by what it does.","blocks":[["p","On macOS, use [[⌘]] wherever this page says [[Ctrl]]."],["p","Inside the app, press [[?]] for the same list with a search field. Shortcuts with a single key do nothing while you are typing in a field."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-shortcuts.webp","The keyboard shortcuts overlay, with search."],["h","File"],["keys",[["Ctrl + N","New scene, in a new tab"],["Ctrl + O","Open a file"],["Ctrl + Shift + O","Import a file into the open scene"],["Ctrl + S","Save the scene"],["Ctrl + E","Export"]]],["h","Edit"],["keys",[["Ctrl + Z","Undo"],["Ctrl + Y","Redo"],["Ctrl + Shift + Z","Redo"],["Ctrl + C","Copy the selected parts"],["Ctrl + V","Paste"],["Ctrl + D","Duplicate the selection"],["Del","Delete the selection. Backspace does the same."]]],["h","Tools"],["keys",[["X","Split"],["P","Fill holes"],["Ctrl + B","Smart fit"],["Ctrl + M","Merge the selected parts into one"],["Ctrl + G","Group"],["Ctrl + Shift + G","Ungroup"],["F2","Batch rename"],["M","Measure"],["L","Draw lines and shapes"],["Enter","Run the open command panel"],["Esc","Put the open command panel away"]]],["p","Decimate, Fit to budget and Select hidden parts have no key of their own. Find them on the toolbar, in the `…` list or with the search."],["h","Selection"],["keys",[["Ctrl + A","Select all"],["Ctrl + I","Invert the selection"],["Esc","Select nothing"],["Shift + S","Find the selected part in the tree"],["Ctrl + Click","In the tree: add a row to the selection or take it out. A group goes in or out with its parts."],["Shift + Click","In the tree: pick every row from the last one you clicked to this one, groups and parts alike."]]],["h","Visibility"],["keys",[["H","Hide the selection"],["Shift + H","Hide the rest"],["Alt + H","Show all"],["S","Isolate the selection, and back"]]],["h","View"],["keys",[["F","Frame the selection, or the whole model"],["1","Solid"],["2","Wireframe"],["3","X-ray"],["4","Heatmap"],["5","Clay, and back to solid"],["Ctrl + 1","Camera view (perspective)"],["Ctrl + 2","Top view"],["Ctrl + 3","Front view"],["Ctrl + 4","Side view"],["G","Show or hide the floor grid"]]],["h","Move, rotate, scale"],["keys",[["E","Move"],["R","Rotate"],["T","Scale"],["Q","Hide the gizmo"],["Shift","Hold while dragging the gizmo to snap"]]],["h","Panels and windows"],["keys",[["Ctrl + K","Search commands, parts and the library"],["Shift + M","Materials dock"],["`","Console"],["Ctrl + ,","Settings"],["Ctrl + ;","Scene settings"],["?","Keyboard shortcuts"],["Ctrl + F","In Settings: jump to the search field"],["Esc","Close the open window or menu"]]],["h","Mouse, in the viewport"],["keys",[["Click","Select a part"],["Shift + Click","Add to the selection"],["Ctrl + Click","Add to or remove from the selection"],["Ctrl + Drag","Box select"],["Ctrl + Shift + Drag","Box select, adding to the selection"],["Drag","Orbit"],["Middle drag","Pan"],["Right drag","Pan"],["Scroll","Zoom"],["Alt + Right drag","Zoom smoothly"],["Right-click","Menu for the part or the view"]]],["h","Mouse, elsewhere"],["keys",[["Double-click","On a name in the tree or on a tab: rename"],["Shift + Click","In the tree: select a range of rows"],["Middle click","On a tab: close it"],["Esc","While dragging a tab: put it back"],["Shift","While dragging a number: ten times faster"],["Alt","While dragging a number: ten times finer"]]],["see",["search-and-commands","move-the-view","select-parts"]]]},{"slug":"command-line","group":"reference","title":"The command-line converter","summary":"Convert STEP files to GLB without opening the viewer. How to run step2glb.py, and what every option does.","blocks":[["p","The converter is a Python script, `step2glb.py`. The app runs it for you when you drop a STEP file on the window. You can also run it yourself in a terminal, for example to convert many files, or to use options the import window does not show."],["p","You never need this page to use the app. It is here for when you want more control."],["h","Before you start"],["steps",["Start the app once with its launcher, so the Python environment exists. See [Start it the first time](#first-start).","Open a terminal in the app folder. On Windows: open the folder in File Explorer, click the address bar, type `cmd` and press [[Enter]]. On macOS: open Terminal, type `cd` and a space, drag the app folder onto the window, and press [[Enter]].","Switch on the app’s Python environment. This makes the command `python` use the add-ons the launcher installed. On Windows, type the first command below. On macOS, type the second."]],["code",".venv\\Scripts\\activate"],["code","source .venv/bin/activate"],["h","Convert a file"],["code","python step2glb.py input.step"],["p","Replace `input.step` with the path of your file. The result is written next to it, with the same name and the ending `.glb`. The terminal prints what the converter is doing."],["h","Examples"],["terms",[["`python step2glb.py input.step --quality 0.2 --min-size 0.5`","A finer tessellation, and parts smaller than 0.5% of the model left out."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --relative`","Quality as a fraction of the model’s size, so it does not depend on the file’s units."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --meshopt`","Compress the result with gltfpack."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --simplify 0.5`","Also halve the triangle count. Lossy."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --no-colors --no-instance`","The plain reader: no colours, no instancing."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --out small.glb --target-tris 200000`","Adjust the quality automatically until the result has at most 200,000 triangles."],["`python step2glb.py a.step b.step c.step`","Convert several files, one after the other."]]],["h","Input and output"],["terms",[["`input`","One or more STEP files."],["`--out`, `-o`","The file to write. Only with a single input file. Without it, the GLB is written next to the input."],["`--force`","Convert again even if an output that is newer than the input already exists. Without it, the converter skips a file that was already converted with the same settings."]]],["h","Mesh quality"],["terms",[["`--quality`, `-q`","How closely the triangles follow the true surface (the linear deflection). Smaller is finer and heavier. The default is 0.5, in the units of the file, normally millimetres."],["`--relative`","Reads `--quality` as a fraction of the model’s bounding-box diagonal instead of an absolute distance."],["`--angular`","The angular deflection, in radians: a second limit on how coarsely curved surfaces are cut into triangles. The default is 0.5, about 28.6°."],["`--min-size`","Leave out parts whose size is below this percentage of the model’s diagonal. The default, 0, keeps everything."]]],["h","Budgets"],["terms",[["`--target-tris`","Adjust `--quality` automatically to land at or under this triangle count. The conversion is run again up to five times."],["`--target-size-mb`","The same, for the size of the output file in megabytes. With both set, the tighter one wins."]]],["h","Repeated parts"],["terms",[["`--no-instance`","Do not collapse identical shapes into instances. According to the README, it only has an effect together with `--no-colors`."],["`--pca-instances`","An experimental, more aggressive way to find repeated shapes. It can turn symmetric parts the wrong way."]]],["h","Colours, names and the tree"],["terms",[["`--no-colors`","Use the plain reader: geometry only, no colours, names or hierarchy. The fastest."],["`--force-colors`","Always read colours, names and the hierarchy. Without this option, the converter skips them for files over 1 GB."],["`--no-shuo`","Skip per-instance colour overrides. A big saving in time on assemblies with many repeated parts."],["`--no-layers`","Skip CAD layer information."],["`--no-materials`","Skip material information."],["`--no-step-names`","Skip product names. Parts get generic names in the tree."],["`--no-step-props`","Skip validation properties such as mass and area."],["`--props`","Work out the volume and surface area of every solid. Slow on big files."]]],["h","Compression and reduction"],["p","These options use gltfpack, a separate free tool that has to be installed and on your PATH. Without it they are skipped, with a warning."],["terms",[["`--meshopt`","Compress the GLB with Meshopt, for files roughly ten times smaller. This is on by default whenever gltfpack is found."],["`--no-meshopt`","Switch that default off."],["`--quantize`","Store positions, normals and texture coordinates as smaller numbers."],["`--simplify`","Keep this fraction of the triangles, between 0 and 1. 0.5 halves the count. Sharp edges are preserved. Lossy. It switches Meshopt on."]]],["h","Speed"],["terms",[["`--parallel`","Use this many worker processes for building the meshes. The default, 0, does one at a time."]]],["h","Without typing"],["p","The folder also holds `step2glb.bat` (Windows) and `step2glb.command` (macOS). Drag a STEP file onto one of them to convert it with the default settings, without opening the viewer."],["h","Options of the server"],["p","The launcher starts `serve.py`. Started by hand, it takes these options:"],["terms",[["`--tab`","Open the app in an ordinary browser tab instead of a window of its own."],["`--no-browser`","Start the server and open nothing. Type the printed address into a browser yourself."],["`--open`, `-o`","Open this file at start. Accepts STEP, GLB and glTF files. For a STEP file it asks about quality first."],["`--port`","Use this port instead of 4242."]]],["see",["first-file","files-in-folder","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"file-formats","group":"reference","title":"File formats","summary":"Which files the app opens and which it writes, and what each format keeps: the parts tree, materials and vertex colours.","blocks":[["p","This page follows the format sheet inside the app. Open it there with {{Compare}} in the Export window."],["h","Formats it opens"],["terms",[["STEP (.step, .stp)","The CAD exchange format. Converted by the local Python converter. Keeps the parts tree and materials. Open only: the app does not write STEP."],["IGES (.iges, .igs)","An older CAD exchange format, often a surface model. Converted by the local Python converter. Keeps the names, colours and layers the file has, but not an assembly tree. Open only."],["BREP (.brep, .brp)","The OpenCascade shape format. Converted by the local Python converter. Geometry only. Open only."],["GLB","Binary glTF. Keeps the parts tree, full materials and vertex colours. The best all-round format."],["glTF","The text form of GLB. The same capabilities, larger."],["FBX","A common exchange format for Maya, Blender, Unreal and 3ds Max. Keeps the parts tree and materials."],["OBJ","Universal, but with no transforms and no tree. Materials come from a separate `.mtl` file."],["3MF","A 3D-printing format. Keeps the parts tree and materials. Open only."],["STL","Geometry only, as a single mesh. Used for 3D printing."]]],["p","STEP, IGES and BREP files go through the converter. The others load directly in the browser."],["h","Formats it writes"],["terms",[["GLB","Parts tree, full materials, vertex colours. Optional Draco compression."],["glTF","The same as GLB, as text."],["FBX","Parts tree and materials. Binary FBX 7.4 by default, with a text (ASCII) version as a fallback."],["USDZ","Parts tree and materials. For Apple Quick Look and Reality Composer. Write only."],["OBJ","A flat list of parts, with the hierarchy folded into their names. Materials in a `.mtl` file."],["STL","One mesh, geometry only."],["PLY","One mesh, with a colour for every vertex. Write only."],["CSV","A parts list. No geometry. Write only."]]],["h","The three columns of the sheet"],["terms",[["Hierarchy","Whether the format keeps the parts tree: groups, and groups inside groups."],["Materials","Colour and shading for each part. “PBR” means the full set with metalness and roughness. A plain dot means colour only."],["Vertex colours","A colour stored on every corner point of the mesh, with no separate material."]]],["see",["export","choose-format","first-file"]]]},{"slug":"files-in-folder","group":"reference","title":"Files in the folder","summary":"What the files and folders inside the MeshOptimiser folder are for, which ones you use, and which ones to leave alone.","blocks":[["p","You only ever need to double-click one file: the launcher for your system. The rest is the app itself."],["h","The ones you use"],["terms",[["`start.bat`","The launcher for Windows. Double-click it to start the app."],["`start.command`","The launcher for macOS."],["`start_hidden.vbs`","Windows: starts the app like `start.bat`, without showing the launcher window."],["`step2glb.bat`, `step2glb.command`","Convert a STEP file that you drag onto them, without opening the viewer."],["`test-converter.bat`, `test-converter.command`","Run the converter on a STEP file that you drag onto them and show everything it prints. Useful when a conversion fails and you want to see why."],["`README.txt`","A short quick start, in plain text."]]],["h","Folders the app creates"],["terms",[["`.venv`","The app’s own Python environment, created on the first run. It can take up to about 2 GB. It is safe to delete: the launcher builds it again, which takes a few minutes. On macOS the folder is hidden in Finder, because its name starts with a dot."],["`inbox`","Where STEP files are converted. The finished GLB files stay here."]]],["note","The `inbox` folder is not emptied automatically, so it grows with every conversion. With the app closed, you can delete the files in it that you no longer need."],["h","The app itself"],["terms",[["`serve.py`","The small local server. It hands the app’s pages to your browser and starts the converter when you drop a STEP file."],["`step2glb.py`","The converter from STEP to GLB."],["`index.html`, `app-v2.js`","The viewer: the page and its program."],["`holefill.js`","The hole filler behind Fill holes."],["`mesh-worker.js`","A background worker for Fill holes and Decimate, so the window stays usable while they run."],["`cloner.js`","A cloner that makes linear, grid and radial arrays of a part."],["`vendor`","The libraries the viewer needs, bundled so that it starts without the internet."],["`assets`","The app’s icons, pictures and help clips."],["`manifest.webmanifest`","What lets Chrome and Edge install the page as an app."],["`requirements.txt`","The list of Python add-ons the launcher installs: cadquery-ocp, trimesh and numpy."],["`tests`","The self-tests. See [Self-test](#self-test)."],["`docs`, `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`","Screenshots, the project’s description, and the list of changes in every version."]]],["note","Do not open `index.html` by double-clicking it. The page then says it needs a local server. Always start with the launcher."],["see",["first-start","command-line","stop-restart-update"]]]},{"slug":"privacy-offline","group":"reference","title":"Privacy and offline use","summary":"MeshOptimiser runs on your own computer. What stays local, when the internet is used, and what the browser remembers.","blocks":[["h","Your files stay on your computer"],["p","MeshOptimiser is not an online service. The viewer runs in your browser, and the converter runs in Python on your machine. The local server answers only on your own computer, at an address that starts with `localhost`."],["p","When you drop a STEP file, the page hands it to that local server, which converts it in the app’s `inbox` folder. The file does not go anywhere else. There is no account and no sign-in."],["h","When the internet is used"],["terms",[["The first start","The launcher downloads the Python add-ons the converter needs (cadquery-ocp, trimesh, numpy). If Python itself is missing and you agree, it downloads Python too."],["Every later start","Nothing. The libraries the viewer needs are bundled in the `vendor` folder, so the app opens, edits and exports with no connection at all."],["A few rarely used converters","Fetched from the internet the first time they are needed: the transcoder for compressed textures, and the converters behind Draco export and some older FBX files."]]],["h","What the browser remembers"],["p","The app keeps a few things in your browser’s storage, on this computer:"],["list",["your settings;","the list of recent files, with a small picture of each;","permission to reopen a recent file without asking where it is;","the sizes of the sidebars and docks."]],["p","You can clear each of these under {{Settings}}, {{Storage}}. Because they live in the browser’s profile, they survive an update of the app, and they are separate for each browser you use."],["h","What is written to disk"],["list",["Converted GLB files, in the `inbox` folder.","Scenes you save and files you export, where you choose.","The Python environment, in `.venv`."]],["see",["requirements","files-in-folder","settings"]]]},{"slug":"troubleshooting","group":"help","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Fixes for the usual problems: Python not found, a blocked launcher on macOS, missing modules, a blank view and slow conversions.","blocks":[["p","Find the heading that matches what you see. If nothing here helps, report the problem at `https://github.com/xpayn3/MeshOptimiser/issues`. Say which version you have (click the app’s mark in the top bar) and paste the log: {{Copy log}} on the progress card, or the copy button in the console."],["h","“python is not on PATH” on Windows"],["p","Windows cannot find Python. Run the Python installer again and tick “Add Python to PATH”. Or close and reopen the terminal or the launcher, so the new PATH is picked up. See [What you need](#requirements)."],["h","“Operation not permitted” on macOS"],["p","Right-click `start.command` and choose “Open”. macOS blocks a double-click on a freshly unzipped script the first time."],["h","“ModuleNotFoundError: No module named ‘OCP’”"],["p","The Python environment is incomplete. The same goes for a missing `numpy` or `trimesh`. Quit the app, delete the folder `.venv` inside the app folder, and start the launcher again. It rebuilds the environment from scratch."],["h","The first run stops while installing"],["list",["Check your internet connection. The first run has to download the add-ons.","Check your Python version. The converter needs 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. With 3.13 the add-ons cannot be installed. Install a supported version, delete the folder `.venv` if one was created, and start again.","Check that the drive has about 2 GB free."]],["h","“This page needs a local server”"],["p","You opened `index.html` directly. Browsers do not let the app run that way. Start it with `start.bat` or `start.command`, and use the address the launcher prints, normally `http://localhost:4242`."],["h","The app window is empty, or the browser cannot reach the page"],["p","The local server is not running. This happens when you open the installed app or a bookmark without starting the launcher first, or after you closed the launcher window. Start the launcher again."],["h","The address is not localhost:4242"],["p","Something else on your computer was already using port 4242, so the server chose another one. That is fine. The launcher window prints the address it is using."],["h","The app opened in a normal browser tab"],["p","The launcher found no Chrome, Edge or Brave, so it used your default browser. The app works the same in a tab."],["h","The 3D view is empty or looks wrong"],["list",["Press [[F]] to frame the model, and [[Alt + H]] to show hidden parts.","The viewer uses WebGPU when the browser has it and falls back to WebGL2 when it does not. To switch by hand, open {{Settings}}, {{Performance}} and set {{Renderer}} to WebGL2. The page reloads.","If a message says the GPU was lost, reload the page.","Update your browser and your graphics driver."]],["h","A STEP conversion takes very long"],["p","Large assemblies take time, and the reading of names, colours and the tree is the slowest part. The progress card keeps showing the converter’s latest message while it works. To make it faster, change the import settings:"],["list",["Leave {{SHUO overrides}}, {{Layer attributes}} and {{Validation properties (mass, area)}} off.","Choose a coarser {{Tessellation quality}}.","Set {{Drop parts smaller than (% of model)}} above 0 to skip tiny fasteners.","For a huge file where you only need the shape, choose geometry only under {{Colours & materials}}."]],["p","{{Cancel}} on the progress card stops a conversion that is running."],["h","“Conversion failed”"],["p","Press {{Copy log}} on the progress card and read the last lines. To see everything the converter prints, drag the STEP file onto `test-converter.bat` or `test-converter.command`. Include the log when you report the problem."],["h","The model has no colours or no parts tree"],["p","A STEP file may have been converted with geometry only. Open it again and choose the option that reads colours, names and hierarchy under {{Colours & materials}}. A GLB, FBX, OBJ or STL file can only show what the file itself contains."],["h","The view stutters on a heavy model"],["list",["Open {{Settings}}, {{Performance}}. Keep {{Skip tiny parts}} and {{Lower the resolution if it stutters}} on, and try {{Quality}} on Low.","Make the model lighter. See [A sensible order to work in](#workflow)."]],["h","Small parts disappear while I turn the view"],["p","That is {{Skip tiny parts}} at work. They come back the moment the view stops. Switch it off under {{Settings}}, {{Performance}} if you prefer."],["h","A recent file asks me to pick it again"],["p","The browser needs a fresh permission before the app may read that file. Open it once more with {{Open file…}}, or drop it on the window."],["h","The exported model is the wrong size or lies on its side"],["p","Export again with a different {{Unit scale}} or the other {{Up axis}}. See [Export](#export)."],["h","An export fails on a very large model"],["p","Text formats such as OBJ can run out of memory. Use GLB or STL, which are binary."],["h","I closed the window and the app is still running"],["p","Closing the window does not stop the server. Close the launcher window too, or next time use {{Menu}}, then {{Quit app}}."],["see",["requirements","first-start","faq","self-test"]]]},{"slug":"faq","group":"help","title":"Frequently asked questions","summary":"Short answers: cost and licence, privacy, browsers, file sizes, whether Python is needed, and where to report a bug.","blocks":[["h","Is it free?"],["p","Yes. MeshOptimiser is free and open source."],["h","What is the licence?"],["p","MIT. In short, you may use, copy and change it, also for commercial work, as long as the licence text stays with it. It comes with no warranty."],["h","Can I send the exported file to a customer?"],["p","Yes, once you have taken out what they should not see. See [How to make a CAD model safe to send to a customer](#make-model-safe-to-send)."],["h","Does my file leave my computer?"],["p","No. The viewer and the converter both run on your machine. See [Privacy and offline use](#privacy-offline)."],["h","Do I need an internet connection?"],["p","Only for the first start, which downloads the Python add-ons. After that the app runs offline. A few rarely used converters are fetched the first time they are needed."],["h","Which browsers work?"],["p","A recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. The viewer uses WebGPU and falls back to WebGL2. For the app to open in a window of its own, the launcher needs Chrome, Edge or Brave. Installing it as an app is offered by Chrome and Edge."],["h","Which systems does it run on?"],["p","Windows and macOS."],["h","Can I use it without Python?"],["p","No. The app is served by a small Python program on your computer, and STEP files are converted in Python. On Windows, the launcher offers to install Python for you if it is missing."],["h","Which Python version?"],["p","3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. Version 3.13 is not supported yet."],["h","How big a file can it handle?"],["p","There is no fixed limit in these pages, because it depends on your computer’s memory and graphics card. The app is tested on assemblies of thousands of parts and millions of triangles. The README’s example is a 320 MB STEP file. The local server accepts STEP files up to 4 GB."],["h","Does it change my original file?"],["p","No. Nothing reaches a file until you save a scene or export, and both write a new file."],["h","Can it open SolidWorks, Inventor, Creo or CATIA files?"],["p","Not the files those programs save for themselves. Export a STEP file from your CAD program first. See [Native CAD files: export a STEP file first](#cad-files-step-first)."],["h","Can it write STEP files?"],["p","No. It reads STEP and writes mesh formats: GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL and PLY."],["h","Where is the converted file?"],["p","In the folder `inbox` inside the app folder. To put a file where you want it, save a scene or export."],["h","Can I undo everything?"],["p","Every editing action can be undone with [[Ctrl + Z]], and {{Revert to source…}} in the {{Menu}} reloads the file as it was. See [Undo and redo](#undo-redo)."],["h","Why do I have to start it with a launcher?"],["p","Because the converter is a Python program, and a web page cannot start one. The launcher starts a small local server that connects the two."],["h","How do I update?"],["p","Download the new zip and unpack it into a new folder. See [Stop, restart and update](#stop-restart-update)."],["h","Where do I report a bug or ask for a feature?"],["p","At `https://github.com/xpayn3/MeshOptimiser/issues`. The app’s mark in the top bar has a link called {{Report an issue}}. Say what you did, what you expected and what happened, and add the version number and the log."],["see",["troubleshooting","privacy-offline","what-it-is"]]]},{"slug":"self-test","group":"help","title":"Self-test","summary":"For the curious: the app can test itself. One address runs a suite of checks inside the live app and reports the results.","blocks":[["p","You do not need this to use the app. It is useful when you suspect that something is broken on your computer, or before you report a bug."],["h","Run it"],["steps",["Start the app.","Open the address below in the browser. If your launcher printed a different port, use that number.","The app starts driving itself: it works the parts tree, the toolbar, the windows and the right-click menu the way a user would.","Results appear in a panel at the bottom right, and in the console."]],["code","http://localhost:4242/?selftest"],["h","What it checks"],["p","Undo and redo, exports, saving a scene and opening it again, and more. Nothing is downloaded to your computer: the exports are captured in memory."],["h","Run part of it"],["p","Add a word after `selftest=` to run only the tests whose name contains it. This address runs the tests about groups:"],["code","http://localhost:4242/?selftest=groups"],["h","The hole filler has its own test"],["p","It needs Node.js, a separate program for running JavaScript, and nothing else. In a terminal in the app folder:"],["code","node tests/holefill.test.mjs"],["p","It builds plates, pockets, counterbores, engraved letters, a boss, a washer and a hollow box, runs the hole filler on them, and checks that the result is closed, has the right volume, and is untouched where it should be."],["see",["troubleshooting","fill-holes","command-line"]]]}]};
